\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Mixed; Yield Curve Flattens\b0\par\par\uc2 1,960 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2011\par 04:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds are mixed as shorter-end bonds fall, while longer-end bonds rise. "Shorter-end bonds are weighed as high inflation data highlights the possibility that the Bank of Korea may hike rates at its August policy meeting," a bond manager at a local bank. Government data showed earlier Monday South Korea's CPI rose 4.7% on year in July compared with a 4.4% rise tipped by economists in a Dow Jones poll. Meanwhile, longer-end bonds rise amid growing worries over the global economic slowdown, stemming from the weak U.S. economy, after Friday's weak U.S. 2Q GDP. "The agreement in Washington to raise the U.S. debt ceiling requires a tighter fiscal policy ahead, which could lead to an even slower economy," the bond manager says. He tips the three-year yield in a 3.85%-3.90% band, while it's now up 3 bps at 3.88%. The five-year yield is flat at 4.02%, while the 10-year is down 1 bp at 4.19%. September bond futures are down 6 ticks at 102.84. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold prices could fall to $1,550 a troy ounce in the near term amid better risk appetite following an agreement in the U.S. to lift the country's debt ceiling, Jonathan Barratt, managing director of Commodity Broking Services in Sydney, says. "We'll see gold take a little breather, it may go back towards $1,550/oz and then we might see a lift," he says. He expects investors to take their cash out of precious metals and move it into equities as the appeal of safe-haven assets fades following the U.S. debt deal. This "euphoric bounce" could last for up to two weeks, he says. Spot gold is at $1,612.20 a troy ounce, down $15 from its previous settlement. Prices fell as much as 1.0% earlier in Asian trading as investors sold gold in favor of riskier assets including base metals.(clementine.wallop@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 1.5% at 22,776.26, after a deal is struck on U.S. debt ceiling talks. Core Pacific-Yamaichi notes the immediate resistance for the HSI will be around 22,800 as there are many major technical resistances at that level, including the peak of HSI on July 2 at 22,835, and the 250-day moving average of 22,872. "The short-term momentum is strong as the market has already oversold," it adds. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) is up 1.9% at HK$78.00 ahead of its 1H11 results after market closes, the stock is also gaining support from news it has struck a deal to sell 195 retail-banking branches to \uc2 First Niagara Financial Group\uc2  (FNFG) for US$1 billion. \uc2 China Resources Enterprise\uc2  (0291.HK) is up 2.5% at HK$34.70, on news it has agreed to buy a department store and supermarket chain for CNY3.69 billion. Volume is modest at HK$9.01 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.4% at 1555.13 in early trade amid strong regional markets after Washington reaches a deal to raise the debt ceiling and avoid a default. A local dealer says this is a relief rally and cautions that investors need to be wary of the recovery in the global economy after weak U.S. GDP data Friday. "There are still issues outside the country. The European debt issues are still very much there and with the disappointing U.S. GDP data, the rally may not last," he says. He tips resistance at 1560. Market breadth is positive with 266 gainers vs 98 losers. \uc2 YTL Power\uc2  (6742.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR1.99, \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR6.95 and CIMB Group (1023.KU) is up 1.2% at MYR8.37. Among losers, \uc2 Hong Leong Bank\uc2  (5819.KU) is down 0.9% at MYR13.22 and \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) is down 0.4% at MYR5.13.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.8% at 3214.06, gaining alongside regional markets after U.S. President Obama confirms that leaders of both parties in Washington have reached an agreement to lift the debt ceiling and reduce the federal deficit. Adding to the positive tone is news China's official PMI falls slightly in July but remains in expansionary mode, at 50.7 from 50.9 in June. "We're reacting mostly to the U.S. debt deal news. Uncertainty over this had been a drag on regional markets and ours for a while," says a trader at a local brokerage. He adds China's PMI is also helping sentiment as the slight decline points to a gradual slowdown, and will lift hopes China's economy can avert a hard landing. Market volume is strong at 374 million shares worth S$379 million with 3.5 gainers for every decliner. Only Jardine Strategic (J37.SG) and \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG) are lower on the STI, after reporting 1H11 results Friday. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) rises 1.8% to S$1.935. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are up a modest 0.3% at 8672.70; while investors are cheered by the agreement on the raising of the U.S. debt ceiling, \uc2 Formosa Plastics Group\uc2  companies' decline by their daily limit, wipes out most of the Taiex's gains. First Capital says developments in the U.S. will support the Taiex above 8560 near term, while 8700 remains a strong resistance largely due to declines in Formosa Group, after the government ordered more closure at their Mailiao complex for safety checks. Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) is down 6.9% at NT$101.00, Formosa Petrochemical (6505.TW) is down 6.9% at NT$101.00, \uc2 Nan Ya Plastics\uc2  (1303.TW) is down 6.9% at NT$74.20, and Formosa Chemical (1326.TW) is down 7.0% at NT$97.20. Some of Formosa's rivals are limit-up: \uc2 China General Plastics\uc2  (1305.TW) is +6.7% at NT$14.35, China Petrochemical (1314.TW) rises 6.9% to NT$47.90. Hon Hai (2317.TW) is +3.2% at NT$84.90, TSMC (2330.TW) rises 0.8% to NT$72.60, HTC (2498.TW) is +1.3% at NT$869.00. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open lower as investors anticipate the release of data next week showing inflationary pressures remained high in July. The Shanghai Index is down 0.4% at 2691.15, with analysts eyeing support at 2650. "Investors aren't that affected by the U.S. agreement on a debt deal, and the PMI data came within market expectations. It's worries about inflation that are pushing stocks lower," says Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. China's official PMI fell to 50.7 in July from 50.9 in June, indicating a slightly slower pace of expansion. Among losers, Bank of China (601988.SH) is 0.7% lower at CNY3.06, \uc2 Wuhan Iron & Steel\uc2  (600005.SH) is down 0.8% at CNY3.91 and Poly Real Estate (600048.SH) has shed 0.2% to CNY10.45. The Shenzhen index is down 0.3% at 1174.78. (andrew.galbraith@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] While the U.S. debt ceiling deal makes Japanese JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely for now, as the agreement has kicked Japanese share prices higher, the longer-term possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is larger than the market expects," says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is looking at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the standpoint of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term competitiveness, including points such as Japanese firms' ability to compete with Korean competitors with the yen strong against the won." If the country's Nikkei Stock Average begins falling again in coming weeks, that would be one possible trigger for Tokyo to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Takashima says. He adds that given its longer-term focus, the government is likely now more concerned over sustained JPY strength, rather than on smoothing out any sudden moves in the unit. The USD/JPY is at 77.84, off an earlier session high of 78.05. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] An agreement to raise U.S. borrowing limits will likely clear the way for more appreciation in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the KRW and the TWD, says Nick Verdi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The USD may enjoy a brief rally once Congress actually passes the deal, but this will be short-lived as the long-term trend of dollar-weakness in Asia will prevail, he says. Meanwhile, the JPY and the CHF are set to weaken: "The strength of the yen and Swiss franc in recent weeks has been a flight to safety in the event the U.S. debt ceiling wouldn't be raised. Once that is removed, dollar weakness will be better expressed through commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Verdi. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] China's official purchasing manager's index in July, which fell slightly to 50.7 from 50.9 in June, was better than market expectations though it's still weak, which could mean Beijing will introduce more policies to boost activities of small and medium-sized enterprises if the easing in production continues, says Ma Jun, an economist at Deutsche bank. The subindex of input prices also declined to 56.3 in July from 56.7 in June, showing that non-food inflation has already stabilized, Ma adds. (yajun.zhang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to open higher, in line with gains in most regional bourses, as an agreement by U.S. congressional leaders to raise the debt ceiling bolsters sentiment and improves risk appetite. "The latest news flow on the U.S. compromised debt deal should be positive for Thai stocks today as foreign inflows will accelerate in our view," says KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul. Resistance is tipped in the 1145-1150 zone, while support is at 1130; Thai shares closed down 0.1% at 1133.53 Friday. Domestically, eyes are on the opening of a parliamentary session today, with official voting for the Prime Minister post likely to take place in a few days. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 31 Jul 2011 22:02 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Mixed; Yield Curve -2-\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are higher after the official CFLP PMI data shows China's manufacturing activity slowed for the fourth consecutive month in July, fueling concerns of a hard landing in the country's economy, says a Shanghai-based trader with a local insurer. "As the CPI growth is very likely to have peaked in June, policy makers may refrain from launching hawkish tightening policies in the second half of the year, which is positive to bond prices," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 128.92 from 128.88 at last close, and is tipped to test resistance at 129.00 later this week. China's official PMI fell to 50.7 in July from 50.9 in June. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 31, 2011 22:02 ET (02:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110801e781000ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
