\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister: Will Act Against Excessive Moves, Not Certain Level\b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2011\par 07:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japanese Finance Minister Noda says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is temporarily effective.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act against excessive yen movement, rather than acting if the yen hits any particular level.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda says monetary policy is an option to deal with yen strength in the long term.\par \par \uc2 (Recasts and adds comments from Noda and other officials throughout.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By George Nishiyama \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister Friday said the authorities will act in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if yen movement becomes excessive, rather than acting if it hits any particular level, as the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit a four-month high against the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a certain level of effectiveness temporarily," Yoshihiko Noda told Parliament. "But that should be against excessive or disorderly movement, and not against a certain level."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0420 GMT, the dollar was trading at around Y77.50, after falling to a four-month low of Y77.48--not far from the post-war low of Y76.25 touched March 17, 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the first time for Noda to touch on the effectiveness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the recent appreciation of the yen, but his comments that authorities didn't have a particular level in mind made it unclear whether they were ready to step in soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also said that taking monetary policy measures may be an option to deal with the strong yen, in response to a lawmaker's comment that the Bank of Japan could do more to deal with the yen which has appreciated over the past 20 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since it is related to deflation, substantial steps (against yen strength) could involve monetary policy, as well as employment and labor policy," Noda said without elaborating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ will hold a two-day meeting of its policy board beginning Thursday. Noda has in the past repeatedly said that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is independent in setting monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior BOJ official attending the same parliamentary committee said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needed to be mindful of both the short- and long-term negative impact of the rising yen on the economy. Noda also said the rising yen didn't reflect the fundamentals of the Japanese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government's top spokesman also sounded a warning against the yen's strength, saying the Cabinet shares the strong concerns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But other government officials have pointed out that the yen's recent appreciation was brought on by concern over talks in the U.S. to increase the amount of debt that country can issue. This has led many market participants to believe that Japanese authorities will wait for the outcome of those talks before taking any action, they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By George Nishiyama, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Mochizuki and Toko Sekiguchi contributed to this report. [ 29-07-11 0504GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110729e77t000ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
