\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Japan escalates warnings on yen rise to protect recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2011\par 04:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finmin Noda says weighing how long Tokyo can keep yen rise "unattended"\par \par \uc2 * Sources say still no full agreement on whether and when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Will take appropriate action in cooperation with BOJ - Noda\par \par \uc2 * BOJ official says focusing on yen rise harm to economy\par \par \uc2 * Factory output up 3.9 pct in June, confirms recovery on track\par \par \uc2 (Updates markets, add sources)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 29 (Reuters) - Japan on Friday escalated its warning to financial markets against testing the yen's upside further, with the finance ministry signalling that Tokyo may not wait for too long with action if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps climbing.\par \par \uc2 In his strongest threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said that the yen was rising "too much" and deviating from Japan's economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "Our stance is clear. We will take decisive action against excessive exchange rate volatility," Noda told parliament.\par \par \uc2 "I'd like to carefully examine how long we can leave current (exchange-rate) moves unattended."\par \par \uc2 Markets feel that Tokyo's verbal warnings have reached a higher pitch and see \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a real possibility, but not until the yen accelerates toward its record high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the yen's rise driven by factors beyond Japan's control such as the U.S. debt crisis, there is still no full agreement among monetary authorities on whether and when to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sources familiar with the government's exchange-rate policy say.\par \par \uc2 The likelihood of action may depend on the outcome of a tussle between government officials sensitive to business complaints who would prefer swift action, and those who work with G7 partners and are hestitant of stepping in too hastily, said one of the sources who spoke on condition of anonymity. Another source confirmed this view.\par \par \uc2 Any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would increase the chance of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  following up with monetary easing, as was the case when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own in September, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 In a nudge towards the Bank of Japan, Noda said he hoped to take appropriate action "in cooperation" with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to address the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise that was hurting exporters and threatening Japan's recovery from damage wrought by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed factory output rose further in June and manufacturers expected more gains in July and August that would bring production close to pre-quake levels, but economists said the yen's rise was clouding the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers' repeated verbal warnings have not prevented the dollar from sliding toward the record low of 76.25 yen\par \par \uc2 struck in March on fears of a U.S. debt default or credit downgrade.\par \par \uc2 It hit another four-month low of 77.48 yen on Friday morning after Republican Representative Kevin McCarthy said the House of Representatives will delay a vote to raise the U.S. debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 A senior BOJ official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was focusing on how recent yen rises could affect a still fragile economic recovery, suggesting its readiness to ease monetary policy further as early as next week if the yen climbs further.\par \par \uc2 SOLO ACTION\par \par \uc2 Markets rule out a repeat of the co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the Group of Seven carried out in the aftermath of the March quake, but some see solo action by Tokyo as a possibility.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers, alarmed at the persistent nature of yen rises amid broad-based weakness in the dollar, see solo action as an increasingly viable option, although markets are sceptical how long its effect would last.\par \par \uc2 "Noda's verbal warning has escalated slightly," said Michiyoshi Kato, senior vice president at \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's not about dollar/yen levels alone. Authorities are probably also watching stock markets. If the U.S. debt problem triggers risk aversion and pushes the yen up suddenly, and if that increases worries about the impact on Japan's economy, Tokyo may act."\par \par \uc2 Policymakers who are hesitant about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have pointed to the resilience of the stock market as a sign that the damage to the economy has been contained so far.\par \par \uc2 But the Nikkei average fell below the psychologically important 10,000 mark on Friday and business lobbies have started to complain more vocally about the government's inaction over the strong yen. Noda said he was aware of business concerns.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ feels the yen rise has yet to severely undermine business sentiment but is prepared to ease policy further next week if the standoff in U.S. debt talks roils global markets.\par \par \uc2 "We need to watch out for the negative impact yen rises could have on the economy through exports, corporate revenues and a worsening of business sentiment," BOJ Executive Director Masayoshi Amamiya told parliament on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy is expected to exit recession and grow moderately in July-September as companies make steady progress restoring supply chains hit by the quake.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Stanley White, Rie Ishiguro and Kaori Kaneko; Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/(WRAPUP 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110801e77t00031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
