\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Push By Corporate  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  For Weaker Yen Ratchets Up  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Chances\b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2011\par 07:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japanese exporters increasingly vocal on damage caused by strong yen\par \par \uc2 -- Noda steps up warnings on the yen, but impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in doubt\par \par \uc2 -- Any yen selling would likely be solo, making success more elusive\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--With Japanese exporters increasingly demanding an end to the recent sharp gains in the yen, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers say pressure is growing on the government to launch yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , although the chances of success are seen as uncertain at best.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has been falling against the yen as the standoff over the U.S. debt ceiling drags on in Washington. As of 0515 GMT it was at Y77.84, nearing its post-war record low of Y76.25.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo traders believe that Japanese authorities are primarily looking at market volatility in trying to gauge the timing of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But an increasingly weak dollar, such as a fall to Y76.00 could also be a trigger, due to the impact on Japan's important exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The current strong yen is beyond what any individual company can deal with," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joji Tagawa\f28 \uc2 \f2 , vice president at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., said Wednesday as the company announced that its profits dropped 20% in the April-June quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, who acknowledges the concerns of exporters, said Sunday the ministry "will take decisive action when necessary," seen as his most serious warning yet about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With that warning issued, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may step into the market anytime soon. But they said pushing the yen sharply lower will be difficult.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducts \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now, the impact will be blunted by the record-high level of short-yen positions held by retail margin traders. These investors are expected to take profits on any yen plunges that result from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, if the MOF decides to wait for a better time, it may face an increasingly uphill task in trying to get the dollar back above Y80, seen as the minimum level required by exporters to avoid more pain ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's worse, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely won't see other nations joining in any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Tsutomu Soma, a senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Okasan Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In March, other G-7 nations participated in the campaign because of their sympathy for Japan following the earthquake," Soma said. This time around, other nations have their own concerns and are unlikely to assist \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Faced with the difficulties, authorities are aware that they need to catch the market "by surprise" to maximize the impact, a finance ministry official told Dow Jones Newswires earlier this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Soma said one such example would be bundling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's aggressive monetary easing measures, though the conservative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is considered unlikely to go along with such an idea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy board member Hidetoshi Kamezaki said Wednesday he is concerned about the yen's strength but "at this moment, I do not see a need for further credit easing."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another surprise would be to step in during non-Asian hours, traders said. Historically, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has always preferred to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the Asian day, in part because Japanese exporters are most active at that time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Europe or New York is theoretically possible, but we think it's hardly likely," a senior trader at a big fund in Tokyo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 28-07-11 0557GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110728e77s000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
