\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: JGBs Set To Rise On Euro-Zone Debt Fears, Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2011\par 01:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open higher amid continuing concerns over euro-zone sovereign debt problems, ensuing yen appreciation and expected stock weakness, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa. Month-end demand is also expected to support the market, though players are likely to become cautious about chasing prices higher if the 10-year yield declines to around 1.05%, she adds. Lead September JGB futures are tipped in a 141.50-141.70 band vs 141.55 at Monday's close, while the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield is eyed in a 1.070%-1.090% range vs 1.085%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are mixed, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.5% at 4535.0 despite a lack of progress on the U.S. debt ceiling, which saw Wall Street's S&P 500 fall 0.6% to 1337.43. House Speaker John Boehner spelt out his plan to trim as much as US$3 trillion from the U.S. deficit and lift the debt ceiling in a two-step process, while Senate Democrats pursued a separate proposal. European bond markets fell after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut Greece's credit rating three notches deeper into junk territory, warning that the latest financial bailout deal implies a default. S&P 500 Financials fell 0.8%, and Materials fell 0.2%, though BHP Billton (BHP.AU) ADRs rose to equivalent A$43.26 vs A$43.06 locally. Energy fell 0.5%, with Nymex crude oil down 67 cents to US$99.20. London-listed BHP fell 0.6% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) was flat. LME copper fell 0.2%, while aluminum rose 1.2%. Spot gold remained firm near US$1,613.00 after hitting a record high of US$1,620.50 in Asian trading. The S&P/ASX 200 closed Monday at 4530.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 78.28; the pair may remain around that level ahead of U.S. President Obama's speech on debt ceiling problems from 0100 GMT, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. "All eyes are on President Obama, to see whether he will say something positive or negative about the problem," he says. If Obama signals an agreement with the opposition, the EUR may fall as investors' focus will return to European debt issues after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Greece's debt rating on Monday. If Obama fails to suggest any agreement, the USD/JPY will likely fall further; investors will increase their alert level for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though "I personally think the timing (for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not yet there because Japanese share prices are still firm." He tips the USD/JPY in a 78.00-78.75 range. The EUR/USD is at 1.4376; he tips a 1.4325-1.4400 range. The EUR/JPY is at 112.54; he tips a 112.00-113.00 band.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's manufacturing output likely rebounded in June after contracting for two straight months as the effects of a high base of comparison from last year and the disruptions to the supply chain from the March 11 Japan earthquake waned. June manufacturing output likely grew 10.5% from a year earlier, according to the median estimate of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. That compares with a 17.5% on-year contraction reported for May. "The sharp decline in industrial production in April and May was consistent with the soft patch in the global economy. The base effect in June will favor as there was a swing in the same month last year," says David Cohen, an economist at Action Economics. "Meanwhile, Japan's industrial production and exports have shown a welcome rebound and that is likely to filter through as supply chains get replenished," he adds. The government will release the industrial output data for June at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2258 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets are likely to fall amid increasing uncertainty around U.S. debt ceiling negotiations, which curbed investors' risk appetite Monday, leading stocks on Wall Street to close lower. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets investors are likely to remain wary of the USD amid ongoing concerns over debt negotiations in Washington and after the USD/CHF fell to an all-time low of 0.8021 Monday, while the USD/JPY fell to a post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  low of 78.05. Investors will likely look to a speech by U.S. President Barack Obama for cues; Obama is set to discuss the political stalemate over slashing the country's deficit and raising the debt ceiling. The EUR/USD is at 1.4373 from 1.4377 late Monday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 112.53 from 112.57, and the USD/JPY is at 78.29 from 78.27. On the data slate U.S. President Obama speaks on debt ceiling problems at 0100 GMT, RBA Governor Glenn Stevens speaks to the Anika Foundation at 0305 GMT, there's Singapore's June industrial production at 0500 GMT and the RBI's annual statement on monetary policy quarterly review at 0600 GMT. In the U.K. there's 2Q GDP, and in the U.S. there's June new residential sales and July consumer confidence, while Kansas City Fed President Hoenig testifies on QE3 and monetary policy. (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may trade in a range between 10,000-10,100 vs Monday's close of 10,050.01 (down 0.8%) as sentiment continues to be weighed down by the firm yen (USD/JPY 78.29, EUR/JPY 112.54). "There continues to be a very strong sense of caution and uncertainty surrounding the U.S. debt talks," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at SMBC Nikko Securities. The impasse on the U.S. talks continued as Republicans and Democrats each pledged separate efforts to raise the debt ceiling. Nishi also notes that trading volume in Tokyo has been low as investors await a string of key Japanese corporate earnings due later this week, including those from Fanuc (6954.TO) and \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO). Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 10,070 vs the Osaka close at 10,050. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices face modest downward pressure Tuesday after U.S. wheat futures finished lower overnight on spillover pressure from losses in corn and soy markets. Increasing expectations for Russia's grain exports added pressure but prices settled above session lows due to late short covering, traders say. CBOT September wheat fell 3.75 cents to US$6.8850/bushel. AUD/USD is at 1.0850, easing from 1.0876 earlier this morning. On Monday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January next year in New South Wales slipped A$1.50 to settle at A$250.00/metric ton, while those in Western Australia were up 20 cents at A$263.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  rates strategists note the charitable forum where RBA Governor Glenn Stevens will speak at 0305 GMT was used by him in 2009 to drop an easing bias, so today could be an opportunity for another shift in outlook. They prefer a bias toward a small short on bonds into the speech. "Over the past 18 months, the governor has made 15 speeches, with the market selling off on only five of these occasions. The sell-offs, however, have been significantly larger in magnitude, suggesting that the risk rewards are still skewed towards intraday shorts, especially given the low level of yields and the easing bias already priced in." The three-year September spot contract has opened two ticks weaker at 95.60. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2233 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold is slipping early in Asia after touching a record $1623.49/oz Monday on renewed risk aversion as talks to raise the U.S. debt ceiling remain deadlocked. However, analysts say gold prices will continue to rise as long as markets remain in a state of uncertainty. Spot gold is trading at $1,613.70/oz, down $1.10 from New York's informal close. "The break above $1,610/oz eliminated our expectation of a dip toward $1,560/oz in gold and signals an earlier-than-expected move to our targets at $1,635/0z-$1,670/oz," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note. Spot silver has also slipped, and is now at $40.31/oz, down 8 cents from New York's informal close. "A break above $41.08/oz in silver confirms our bullish view toward our initial target at $43.00/oz," BarCap adds. Platinum is up $3 at $1,789/oz and palladium up $1 at $805/oz. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2227 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY should continue to slide given the U.S. debt impasse and any efforts by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  without the U.S. or EU won't succeed, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely to succeed in a wave of broad USD weakness, especially with JPY vols well behaved, and it may be only a matter of time before 78.00 gives way," they say. The pair is now at 78.315. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2223 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD looks good for a test of 1.4485 after the Greek deal in effect is a major bolster for fiscal union in the EU, say \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists. The pair is at 1.4384. "The road ahead looks reasonably clear for extension of the EUR/USD upside. At the same time, the gridlock seen in the U.S. over debt ceiling talks could see the USD undermined versus the likes of the EUR and CHF." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 25, 2011 19:15 ET (23:15 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110725e77p007nu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
