July 6 high). Support at 1.4133 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.4068 (Tuesday's low), then 1.4014 (Monday's low), 1.3951 (July 13 low) and 1.3837 (July 12 low).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade higher. Supported by optimism over euro-zone debt crisis resolution; AUD-USD interest rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by dovish July RBA monetary policy meeting minutes. Aussie vulnerable to 0230 GMT China July HSBC China flash manufacturing PMI data. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia June official reserve assets. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish. Resistance at 1.0785 (July 14 high); breach would target 1.0800 (July 13 high), then 1.1011 (29-year high set May 2). Support at 1.0705 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.0653 (hourly chart), then 1.0596 (Tuesday's low), 1.0556 (Monday's low) and 1.0534 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade higher after hitting 30-year high of 0.8586 this morning. Supported by rising confidence over euro-zone debt crisis resolution, NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD gains tempered by Kiwi sales on rebounding AUD/NZD cross. Data focus: 0300 GMT NZ June credit card statistics. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at psychological round-numbered level of 0.8600; breach would expose upside to next psychological round-numbered levels of 0.8700 up to 0.9000 (previous base set in December 1976). Support at 0.8521 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 0.8497 (hourly chart), then 0.8426 (Tuesday's low), 0.8404 (Monday's low) and 0.8375 (Friday's low).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade higher. Supported by hopes over euro-zone debt crisis resolution; less-dovish-than-expected July BOE MPC meeting minutes. "Given the weak activity data, and downside news on headline inflation prior to the July meeting, the emphasis on more QE might have been expected to have increased. Further QE was not explicitly mentioned, however," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . But GBP/USD gains tempered by GBP sales on rebounding EUR/GBP cross; drop in UK Nationwide consumer confidence index to 51 in June from 55 in May. Data focus: 0830 GMT 2Q BOE trends in lending report, UK June public sector net borrowing, UK June retail sales. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, while stochastics stay elevated at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher GBP/USD trading near-term. Resistance at 1.6176 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 1.6191 (July 14 high), then 1.6203 (55-day moving average, near downtrend line from May 2 high of 1.6737), 1.6240 (100-day moving average) and 1.6261 (June 22 reaction high). Support at 1.6111 (hourly chart), then at 1.6067 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 1.6040 (Tuesday's low), then 1.6003 (Monday's low), 1.5909 (July 13 low) and 1.5780 (July 12 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with risks skewed higher. Supported by CHF sales on recovering EUR/CHF cross as hopes rise for some solution to euro-zone debt crisis at EU leaders' summit today. Data focus: 0615 GMT Swiss June trade balance, 0900 GMT Swiss July ZEW indicator of economic sentiment. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold; negative MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to increasing upside risks. Resistance at 0.8254 (yesterday's high), then at 0.8277 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8330 (July 13 high), then 0.8400 (July 12 high), 0.8415 (projected top of descending channel formed with May 24 high of 0.8892 and June 3 low of 0.8328), 0.8487 (55-day moving average) and 0.8519 (July 8 reaction high). Support at 0.8174 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 0.8149 (Tuesday's low), then 0.8034 (Monday's record low), psychological 0.8000 and 0.7944 (projected base of channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by firmer oil prices (Nymex September crude settled up 54 cents yesterday at $98.40/bbl), BOC monetary policy report saying "some of the considerable monetary policy stimulus currently in place will be withdrawn", signaling \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to resume rate hikes. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada May employment insurance. USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.9454 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.9436 (April 29 bottom), then psychological round-numbered levels of 0.9400 down to 0.9100, then 0.9065 (record low hit on Nov. 7, 2007). Resistance at 0.9512 (yesterday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, targeting 0.9569 (hourly chart), then 0.9601 (Tuesday's high), 0.9635 (Monday's high) and 0.9667 (July 13 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by diminished worries over euro-zone sovereign debt crisis, Japan importer demand. But EUR/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold; negative MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to increasing upside risks. Resistance at 112.93 (July 14 high); breach would expose upside to 113.40 (previous base set May 16), then 113.97 (200-day moving average) and 115.12 (July 11 high, near 55-day moving average). Support at 111.66 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 111.18 (Tuesday's low, then 110.66 (Monday's low), 110.50 (July 13 low) and 109.58 (July 12 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to trade with risks skewed higher. Supported by growing hopes for euro-zone debt crisis resolution. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold; negative MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to increasing upside risks. Resistance at 0.8847 (July 13 high); breach would expose upside to 0.8898 (July 11 high), then 0.9000 (July 8 high), 0.9017 (July 6 high) and 0.9049 (July 5 high). Support at 0.8766 (yesterday's low), then at 0.8754 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8701 (Monday's reaction low), then 0.8664 (200-day moving average) and 0.8606 (May 26 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com [ 20-07-11 2337GMT ]\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 20-07-11 2337GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110720e77k001bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
