\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF: could  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  without warning\b0\par\par\uc2 151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juli 2011\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 14 (Reuters) - A senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  refused to say on Thursday if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to drive down the yen.\par \par \uc2  "I will not comment," the official said when asked if Japanese authorities had acted to curb the yen after its rise to a four-month high. \par \par \uc2  But it was possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market without giving any advance warning, the official told reporters.\par \par \uc2  He was speaking shortly after the dollar briefly jumped nearly 1 yen, at one point hitting a session high of 79.61 yen\par \par \uc2 , with strong bids by a U.S. bank cited by Tokyo traders.\par \par \uc2  It then drifted lower to trade up 0.1 percent at 79.01 yen.  \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Writing by Michael Watson; Editing by Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /MOF (URGENT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110717e77e00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
