\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan issues fresh warning over yen's surge vs dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2011\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finance Minister Noda says watching markets closely\par \par \uc2 * Econ Minister Yosano says markets should set \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels\par \par \uc2 * Yen hits highest vs dollar since March G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Markets see little chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now\par \par \uc2 (Adds Yosano comment, govt monthly report)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, July 13 (Reuters) - Japan issued a fresh warning over the yen's rise to a four-month high on Wednesday with the finance ministry saying recent moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been one-sided.\par \par \uc2 But few expect Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market any time soon with share prices holding up and Japan's economy recovering from the effects of the March 11 earthquake.\par \par \uc2 Escalating worries that contagion from Greece's debt crisis could force more European countries to seek financial aid have pushed the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher against both the dollar and the euro, as investors sought it as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 "I think the movement has been a little one-sided and I will closely watch markets today as well," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda told reporters. Asked whether Tokyo would step into the market, he said: "I can't comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Economics Minister Kaoru Yosano also said volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were undesirable but added that yen levels should be set by markets and that current rises reflected worries over Europe's debt woes, signalling that no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been rising since the release of disappointing U.S. jobs figures last Friday and hit 78.48 to the dollar on the EBS platform on Wednesday. In early afternoon Tokyo trade it was around 79.40 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers are sensitive to a higher yen as it is seen harming the country' export-led economy, which was tipped back into recession by the March disaster.\par \par \uc2 While they hope to keep speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive, Noda has refrained from using the stronger wording that Tokyo will take "decisive action" when needed -- a phrase seen by markets as signalling that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be imminent.\par \par \uc2 NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  FOR NOW\par \par \uc2 The yen spiked to a record high of 76.25 yen to the dollar in the aftermath of the quake on speculation that Japanese firms would repatriate some of their huge foreign assets to pay for reconstruction, triggering joint G7 action to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years last September to safeguard its fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan loosened monetary policy on both occasions.\par \par \uc2 With Japan's economy now recovering steadily from damage inflicted by the quake and coping better than expected with restrictions on electricity use after the loss of power plants -- and with the Nikkei average showing resilience -- market expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been low so far.\par \par \uc2 The government left its assessment of the economy unchanged in a monthly report on Wednesday, toeing the BOJ's view the previous day that growth was picking up.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think there will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Back in March this year and September last year the Nikkei average was below 9,000. It's at a different level now and rising," said Tohru Sasaki, head of Japan rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at JPMorgan Chase Bank in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average plunged 20 percent after the March quake to around 8,230, but climbed briefly above 10,000 in May and again this month on expectations that Japan is on course for a moderate recovery. It ended Wednesday at 9,963.14, up 0.4 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 "The region that's in trouble and needs support is Europe," Sasaki said. "The situation does not warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it doesn't have any incentive to do so now."\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy likely contracted for a third straight quarter in April-June due to the quake but is expected to grow 1.0 percent in the July-September quarter, a Reuters poll showed, as manufacturers make progress restoring supply chains.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ kept monetary policy on hold and raised its assessment of the economy on Tuesday, encouraged by a rebound in factory output and the prospects for recovery in business and household sentiment.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are keeping a close eye on the euro zone debt crisis as it could trigger a sharp enough yen rise to make them consider additional monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa warned on Wednesday that yen rises would hurt Japan's economy in the short term and that power supply constraints, if prolonged, could prompt Japanese companies to shift production overseas.\par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  , which maintains a larger portion of its manufacturing in Japan than other big Japanese automakers, said on Wednesday that it would consider combining three manufacturing units in Japan's northeast to help it weather the strong yen and the aftermath of the March disaster.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kaori Kaneko and Stanley White; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/NODA  (UPDATE 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110716e77d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
