\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Strong Yen Doesn't Mean  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  - Yet\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2011\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Yen again approaching record highs on euro zone and U.S. concerns\par \par \uc2 -- Current moves and levels not yet seen as supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- At some point Japanese authorities would likely have to act given impact of strong yen on already fragile economy\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki and Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The worsening euro zone crisis and signs of a weaker U.S. economy have combined to make Japan the "last man standing" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars, pushing the yen near all-time record highs and raising the specter of Japanese market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell Wednesday to Y78.48, its lowest level since a record Y76.25 in the wake of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, which prompted the the Group of Seven nations to jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for thr first time in over a decade to push down the yen. As of 0600 GMT, the greenback was at Y79.55.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities remain reluctant to wade into the market when the causes of the yen's rise appear to lie largely offshore. But as the strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weighs on Japanese exporters and threatens to drag on the country's already fragile economy, traders and officials agree that Japan cannot stand on the sidelines for too long.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda has made clear that he is no fan of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires on Monday, Noda said that "action should be limited to when there is excessive volatility or disorderly movements."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, Noda said that he is "watching the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market carefully," but did not follow that up with the phrase that "Japan will take decisive action when it becomes necessary," a remark seen by the market as the government's most serious verbal warning and one step away from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chief government spokesman Yukio Edano issued his own warning to traders, saying that the government will "keep a close and strict watch" on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government and the Bank of Japan are already concerned about the economic impact of electricity shortages following the ongoing nuclear power crisis, and a stronger yen is the last thing they now want to see.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts say the dollar will need to fall below Y77.00 and show a clear impact on the stock market in order to warrant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Despite the strong yen, which normally pushes down stocks, the Nikkei Stock Average posted small gains Wednesday, ending 0.4% higher at 9963.14. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  said a fall below the 9000-mark would be a warning sign for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Authorities may also wait to see if officials in Europe can put out the euro zone debt fires. In recent days the euro has been hit by Ireland's downgrade to junk bond status, as well as signs that a new bailout for Greece is in question and that Italy may soon become part of the problem.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will take place anytime soon, but investors should be aware that the tone of Japan's warnings is changing and it may start making the 'decisive step' remark soon," said Kenichiro Ikezawa, a senior fund manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa SB Investments\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's likely that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is getting seriously concerned about the yen's moves," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another issue revolves around who is calling the shots on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy within the Japanese government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While this is traditionally the province of the powerful Finance Ministry, in the current Kan administration, other officials have been key players as well, according to people familiar with the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They said that last September's surprise \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was apparently led by former Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku, and that Noda was a reluctant participant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - By Takashi Mochizuki and Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2784; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 13-07-11 0711GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110713e77d000i5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
