\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2011\par 08:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA GDP GROWTH FASTER THAN EXPECTED, MAY TIGHTEN POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China reported surprisingly robust economic growth for the second quarter, raising the prospect of further tightening to combat inflation. (published 0448 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL: RISK OF SHARP ECONOMIC SLOWDOWN IS 'SMALL' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's overall economic performance is good despite falls in some indicators, and the risk of a sharp economic slowdown is "small," Sheng Laiyun, spokesman for the National Bureau of Statistics, said Wednesday. (published 0244 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MEDVEDEV URGES BUSINESS TO PICK SIDES AHEAD OF POLLS: REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW --Russian President Dmitry Medvedev urged top businessmen to decide who they want to see as the country's next president amid the uncertainty ahead of key polls, a report said Wednesday. (published 0658 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER CALLS YEN MOVES "ONE-SIDED," MUM ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister raised the volume on his warnings against the rising yen Wednesday, calling the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent moves "a little one-sided," while still maintaining a modest tone that suggests \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't in the cards for now. (published 0450 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVERNMENT MAINTAINS VIEW OF ECONOMY IN JULY REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government said the nation's economy is still smarting four months after the March earthquake and tsunami, as electricity supply problems in the wake of the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant accident threaten improvement in production and exports. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ GOV SHIRAKAWA: BOJ ALONE CAN'T BEAT DEFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The governor of the Bank of Japan said Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alone can't fix the nation's entrenched deflation, countering views that it isn't doing enough to reverse a decade and a half of growth-hobbling price falls. (published 0456 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ POLICY BOARD MEMBERS RENEW OPPOSITION TO JGB UNDERWRITING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's two new policy board members Wednesday repeated their opposition to the idea of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly buying Japanese government bonds to help funding for post-quake reconstruction. (published 0107 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: INITIAL ESTIMATE OF CHINA 1H TOTAL NATIONAL FINANCING CNY7.7 TLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An initial estimate of China's total national financing came to CNY7.7 trillion in the first half of 2011, a senior official at the People's Bank of China said Wednesday. (published 0415 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PROPERTY INVESTMENT, SALES STAY STRONG IN JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Property investment and sales in China remained robust last month, indicating that the government is unlikely to ease the various tightening measures aimed at slowing the rapid rise in house prices in the country. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA JUNE JOBLESS RATE 3.3%; JOBS GROW MOST SINCE JULY 2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's unemployment rate held steady in June from the previous month as Asia's fourth-largest economy added the most number of jobs since July 2010, the government said Wednesday. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S JULY CONSUMER SENTIMENT INDEX -8.3% ON JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An index of consumer sentiment in Australia plummeted 8.3% in July from June, marking the lowest reading in the index in two years, as the ramifications of heightened European debt concerns and a controversial carbon tax soiled household views. (published 0046 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S DOWNGRADES IRELAND TO JUNK, CITING BAILOUT NEED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service downgraded Ireland's sovereign-debt ratings into junk territory, saying the country will likely need another round of official financing before it can rely on the private debt market. (published 2208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF SAYS ITALY NEEDS TO CUT PUBLIC DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund said Tuesday that Italy needs to take decisive steps to cut budget deficits amid rising concerns that Europe's debt crisis could infect the continent's third-largest economy. (published 2342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW ZEALAND JUNE FOOD PRICE INDEX UP 1.4% VS MAY, UP 7.5% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's food price index rose 1.4% in June from May and was up 7.5% on the year, Statistics New Zealand said Wednesday. (published 2324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FLAGS POSSIBLE TIGHTENING AT NEXT MEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday flagged a possible tightening at its policy meeting later this month amid concerns about strong capital flows. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK: PHILIPPINES NEEDS TO IMPROVE INVESTMENT CLIMATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines will have to improve governance and open key sectors of the economy to attract more foreign investment, the country representative of the World Bank said Wednesday. (published 0346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR CORRECTS HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: There is scope for more corrective gains above 1.4054 towards the 1.4185 area. A break above 1.4054 is required to build on the recovery off Tuesday's 1.3837 low, but Monday's completion of a bear pennant keeps the downside threat prevalent, limiting the rally to the vicinity of the pennant breakout point at 1.4178. A push below 1.3935 would prompt a setback towards 1.3871, and only below the latter would re-expose the 1.3837 low. (published 0717 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: CONSOLIDATES BENEATH HIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: A rounded bottom formation on the 60-minute chart is the foundation for a recovery towards 129.78. The 129.78 resistance area consists of intraday congestion highs and the 50% Fibonacci retracement level of the 130.91/128.59 decline. The structure of the decline off Tuesday's contract high at 130.91 suggests the 130.91 high is isolated, and only a move above 130.36 would bring it back into the picture. A push below 128.59 would attract further bear pressure towards 128.06 and 127.85. (published 0633 GMT) [ 13-07-11 0655GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110713e77d000g2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
