\par\par \b \uc2 CHARTING MARKETS: Dollar Set To Move Higher Vs Yen, Jawboning Or Not\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Cox, CMT   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2011\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Even after Japan's finance minister said Wednesday the yen's moves had become a "little one-sided" as the dollar continued to fall broadly, the greenback is set up for a move higher with or without the help of jawboning or outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are stealthy, and so are strict imponderables. But mystery has its charms, perhaps especially for technical analysts who discern that the dollar, for all that it's weaker on the day against the yen, is nonetheless set up for a move higher.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the dollar was down about 0.4% against the yen, to trade at Y78.95.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he was "watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carefully," words that some traders took to mean that officials could be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to squash speculative buying of yen--or at least stand guard to continue their efforts in jawboning the yen lower.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in concert with other central banks, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength after the horrific March earthquake.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar is set to move higher on its own: In fact, the dollar formally broke out of a downtrend roughly a month ago, when it drifted above Y80.01 and edged above a falling channel resistance.\par \par \uc2 Although the dollar fell to a downtrend low of Y78.51 earlier this week, it's nonetheless still above that downtrend, which is support, of course.\par \par \uc2 I calculate that a decisive move above Y79.55 would point the dollar to Y81.56 initially, a move that if it comes about, would clinch a technical breakout on the weekly chart.\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Cox, a chartered market technician, is chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires. He was formerly a senior analyst for the Commodity Research Bureau. He can be reached at 212-416-2212, stephen.cox@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (Data by CQG)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 07-13-11 1605ET ]\par \par \uc2 1081 4247   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110713e77d0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
