\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Calls Yen's Rise "Sharp"\b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2011\par 13:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Japanese Finance Minister Noda calls the dollar's fall to Y79.17 earlier in the day "sharp."\par \par \uc2 --Noda says he will closely monitor the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 --Noda's description of the yen's rise is still modest, suggesting risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may still be low.\par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout with more background.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Kosaku Narioka \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister on Tuesday described the yen's rise earlier in the day as "sharp," expressing his displeasure at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's export-damaging appreciation, but he didn't go so far as to threaten market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell roughly two-thirds of a yen to Y79.17 in early London trading hours, the lowest since March 18, when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market with the other Group of Seven major industrialized nations to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later recovered to around Y79.65, but the level is still below Y80.26 in New York late Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think those moves are sharp, so I'd like to watch the market carefully going forward," Noda told reporters on his way out of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns over Europe's intensifying debt crisis drove investors into what they see as low-risk investments, including the yen, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains in the yen make Japanese exports less competitive abroad. That spells trouble for the country's economy, which depends much on overseas demand to completely pull out of a slump caused by the March 11 earthquake, tsunami and subsequent nuclear-plant disaster.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Noda's description of the yen's appreciation is still modest by Japan's standards, suggesting the authorities may still be steps away from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview Monday with Dow Jones Newswires, Noda said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be limited to when there is "excessive volatility" or "disorderly movements" in the yen's exchange rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials often harden their rhetoric in stages to signal to the market how close they are to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell the yen. The strongest warning from them is the phrase "decisive steps," which in general means market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and preceded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unilateral yen-selling in last September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Kosaku Narioka, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 12, 2011 07:08 ET (11:08 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110712e77c0036o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
