\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,932 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2011\par 01:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    79.96-80.37      79.69-80.68 \par EUR/USD   1.4303-1.4435    1.4209-1.4451 \par AUD/USD   1.0551-1.0619    1.0528-1.0634 \par NZD/USD   0.8076-0.8146    0.8035-0.8214 \par GBP/USD   1.6165-1.6253    1.6107-1.6384 \par USD/CHF   0.8396-0.8460    0.8345-0.8474 \par USD/CAD   0.9705-0.9786    0.9664-0.9805 \par EUR/JPY   114.80-115.99    114.65-116.49 \par EUR/GBP   0.8847-0.8919    0.8824-0.8975 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to range-trade as markets await 1630 GMT U.S. FOMC monetary policy announcement - Fed expected to hold rates at ultra-low levels after recent soft U.S. economic data, likely to reiterate no need for another round of quantitative easing when QE2 program ends this month. Focus also on comments from Fed Chairman Bernanke at post-meeting press conference. USD/JPY underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid increased investor risk tolerance (VIX fear gauge eased 5.65% to 18.86), Wall Street's strong gains overnight (DJIA up 0.91%, Nasdaq up 2.19%) as market participants bet Greek PM Papandreou will survive crucial vote of confidence in parliament and Athens will take appropriate action toward averting sovereign-debt default - results this morning show 155 deputies supported motion and 143 voted against it, with two members not casting a vote. USD/JPY also supported by USD demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  import settlements, expectation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; smaller-than-expected 3.8% drop in U.S. May existing home sales (vs forecast for 5.0% drop). But USD/JPY topside limited by Japan exporter sales; weaker USD sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 74.74 vs 75.00 early Tuesday). Other data: 0600 GMT Japan April revised machine tool orders. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as stochastics neutral, MACD flat; inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 80.01-79.96 band (Friday's low-June 10 low); breach would expose downside to 79.69 (June 8 low), then 79.57 (May 5 reaction low) and 78.44 (76.4% Fibonacci correction of advance from March 17 record low of 76.25 to April 6 high of 85.53). Resistance at 80.37 (Monday's high), then at 80.68 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 81.08 (June 15 high), then 81.33 (June 2 high), 81.57 (June 1 high) and 81.77 (May 31 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate. Underpinned by Greek PM Papandreou's survival in parliament no-confidence vote, paving way for new round of austerity measures Greece must take to satisfy demands of its international creditors. EUR/USD also supported by expectations of July ECB rate hike, sovereign demand for euro. But EUR/USD topside limited by profit-taking on euro-longs built up ahead of the Greek vote. Euro sentiment also dented by worse-than-expected drop in German ZEW economic expectations index to minus 9.0 in June from May's unrevised +3.1 (vs minus 4.1 expected). Data focus: 0900 GMT euro-zone April new industrial orders, 1400 GMT euro-zone June FCCI flash consumer confidence indicator. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode. Resistance at 1.4435 (15-day moving average), then 1.4451 (June 15 high); breach would expose upside to 1.4498 (June 14 high), then 1.4552 (June 10 high). Support at 1.4303 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.4209 (hourly chart), then 1.4191 (Monday's low), 1.4127 (Friday's low), 1.4073 (Thursday's low) and psychological 1.4000.\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower. Undermined by by dovish June RBA monetary policy meeting minutes showing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in no rush to tighten policy; concerns that China economic growth may slow as Beijing continues to tighten monetary policy to combat inflation; Aussie sales on soft AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD losses tempered by firmer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 2.15 yesterday at 338.14), increased investor risk appetite, sovereign demand for Aussie, AUD-USD interest rate differential. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics neutral; 5- & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Support at 1.0551 (hourly chart), then at 1.0528 (yesterday's low); breach would target 1.0492 (Monday's low), then 1.0473 (Thursday's low), 1.0437 (May 25 low) and 1.0414 (100-day moving average). Resistance at 1.0619 (yesterday's high), then at 1.0634 (Friday's high) and 1.0651(55-day moving average); breach would expose upside to 1.0715 (June 14 high), then 1.0748 (June 7 high) and 1.0767-1.0773 band (June 6 high-June 3 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to range-trade. Buoyed by improved investor risk appetite, firmer commodity prices, NZD-USD yield gap, Kiwi demand on softer AUD/NZD cross. But NZD/USD topside limited by concerns over China economic slowdown. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode; inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.8076 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8035 (Monday's low), then 0.8011 (Friday's low, matching 55-day moving average), 0.7968 (Thursday's low) and 0.7897 (May 25 low). Resistance at 0.8146 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8214 (June 14 high), then 0.8279 (June 10 high) and 0.8300 (June 9 post-float high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower; spotlight on 0830 GMT UK June BOE MPC meeting minutes. GBP/USD undermined by comments from BOE's Fisher that further quantitative easing remains "very much on the table" should UK economic recovery remain weak, depreciation of sterling probably necessary; higher-than-expected UK public sector net borrowing of GBP17.4 billion in May (vs GBP17.0 billion expected); CBI June industrial trends survey showing balance of +13 of manufacturers saying output will rise in coming 3 months, down from +20 in May; GBP sales on buoyant EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD losses tempered by stronger investor risk appetite. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode. Support at 1.6165 (yesterday's low), then at 1.6107 (Monday's low) and 1.6092 (Friday's low); breach would target 1.6076 (Thursday's low), then 1.6059 (May 24 swing low), 1.6028 (200-day moving average), psychological 1.6000 and 1.5970 (April 1 low). Resistance at 1.6253 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6384 (June 15 high), then 1.6441 (June 14 high), 1.6466-1.6470 band (June 9 high-June 7 high) and 1.6495 (June 1 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate. Undermined by broadly weaker USD undertone; but losses tempered by CHF-funded carry trades amid healthier investor risk appetite. Data focus: 0900 GMT Swiss June ZEW-\uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  indicator of economic sentiment. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode. Resistance at 0.8460 (yesterday's high), then at 0.8474 (hourly chart) and 0.8518 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8551 (June 15 high), then 0.8638 (downtrend line from April 1 high of 0.9340), 0.8662 (May 27 high) and 0.8692 (55-day moving average). Support at 0.8396 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8345 (June 14 low), then 0.8327 (June 7 record low) and psychological round-numbered levels of 0.8300 and 0.8200.\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by firmer commodity & oil prices (expiring July Nymex crude ended up 14 cents yesterday at $93.40/bbl, more actively traded August crude settled up 54 cents at $94.17/bbl); better investor risk tolerance; weaker global USD; stronger-than-expected 1.0% rise in Canada May composite index of leading indicators (vs +0.5% forecast). But CAD sentiment dented by weaker-than-expected 0.3% increase in Canada April retail sales (vs +0.4% forecast). USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics have turned bearish. Support at 0.9705 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9667-0.9664 band (June 15 low-June 1 low), then 0.9652 (May 31 low), 0.9638 (May 20 low) and 0.9601-0.9596 band (May 13 low-May 12 low). Resistance at 0.9786 (hourly chart), then at 0.9805 (yesterday's high) and 0.9849 (Monday's high); breach would target 0.9868 (Friday's high), then 0.9898 (Thursday's high), 0.9911 (200-day moving average) and 0.9973 (March 15 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate. Supported by Greek PM Papandreou's win in parliament's no-confidence vote; Japan importer demand. But EUR/JPY topside limited by Japan exporter sales; profit-taking on long EUR/JPY positions built up ahead of the Greek vote. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode. Support at 114.80 (hourly chart), then at 114.65 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 113.58 (Monday's low), then 113.50-113.41 band (Thursday's low-May 16 reaction low), 112.93 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 106.50-123.32 March 17-April 11 advance) and 110.47 (76.4% retracement). Resistance at 115.99 (100-day moving average); breach would expose upside to 116.49 (June 15 high), then 116.69 (June 14 high), 116.88 (June 10 high) and 117.46 (June 9 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to trade with risks skewe higher. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics have turned bullish; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.8885 this morning. Resistance at 0.8919 (June 10 high); breach would expose upside to 0.8975 (June 8 high), then psychological 0.9000 and 0.9042 (May 5 swing high). Support at 0.8847 (hourly chart), then at 0.8824 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 0.8788 (Monday's low), then 0.8767 (Friday's low), 0.8720 (Thursday's reaction low, near 100-day moving average) and 0.8654 (May 30 low).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com [ 21-06-11 2335GMT ]\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 21-06-11 2335GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110621e76l001bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
