\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN POSTS DEEP TRADE DEFICIT IN MAY ON QUAKE IMPACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan posted a Y853.7 billion trade deficit in May, the government said Monday, the biggest shortfall in nearly two and a half years as the impact of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami continues to inhibit a recovery in the nation's economy. (published 0153 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT EDANO: LOW TRADE NUMBERS EXPECTED DUE TO DISASTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman said Monday that the country's deep trade deficit in May was expected due to the impact of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. (published 0235 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: FITCH: SEE SERIOUS INFLATION PRESSURES IN CHINA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China faces serious longer-term inflationary challenges, and it is important that it uses interest rates to a greater extent to control pressures, an analyst at Fitch Ratings said Monday. (published 0512 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA RAIN THREATENS VEGETABLE OUTPUT, RAISES PRICES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Heavy rain has reduced vegetable output by about 20% from levels a year earlier in key Chinese provinces and pushed grain and vegetable prices higher, posing a threat to government efforts to raise the quantity of domestic produce amid inflationary pressures. (published 0452 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: FITCH: S KOREA BANKS' SHORT-TERM EXTERNAL DEBT BACK ON RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings is closely watching the short-term external debt of South Korean banks as it remains a key factor in determining the nation's sovereign credit ratings, Fitch's Asia Pacific Head of Sovereign Ratings Andrew Colquhoun said Monday. (published 0247 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBI'S GOKARN: TO END RATE HIKES ONCE CORE INFLATION STABILIZES AT 4%-4.5% - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will end its rate-tightening cycle once non-food manufacturing, or core, inflation stabilizes around 4%-4.5%, a report cited Deputy Governor Subir Gokarn as saying. (published 0310 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: Eurogroup Takes Step Forward On Greece; Markets Uncertain \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone finance ministers on Sunday took a step toward shoring up financially troubled Greece, but until the ink is dried on a final agreement, markets are likely to trade in choppy waters. (published 0201 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN FINANCE MINISTER: PRIVATE-SECTOR INVOLVEMENT IN GREEK BAILOUT MUST BE VOLUNTARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble stressed Monday that involvement of the private sector in a second bailout for Greece must be voluntary. (published 0544 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF: DUBAI, ABU DHABI MAY FACE FUNDING COST SHOCK AMID GRE DEBTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 High debt levels among Dubai and Abu Dhabi government related entities, or GREs, present a significant risk to the sovereign balance sheets of both emirates, which may face funding cost "shocks" as they attempt to roll over $60 billion worth of debt maturing this year and next, according to a new International Monetary Fund report. (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MAY RAISE INTEREST RATES 2-3 MORE TIMES THIS YEAR - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China may raise interest rates another two or three times this year to contain rising inflation, the state-run Shanghai Securities News reported Monday, citing a senior government think-tank official. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAILAND SWINGS BACK TO TRADE SURPLUS; BOT TIPPED TO HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's trade balance swung back to a surplus of $277.5 million in May, with ongoing export growth likely to encourage the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  to continue its monetary policy tightening efforts to curb accelerating inflation. (published 0446 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW ZEALAND PM ESTIMATES EARTHQUAKE COSTS AT 8-9% OF GDP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand Prime Minister John Key Monday said his government estimates recent earthquakes in the country will cost between 8% and 9% of national output. (published 0256 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BEIJING ENTERPRISES WATER PLANS YUAN-DENOMINATED BOND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beijing Enterprises Water Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it plans to offer fixed rate, yuan-denominated bonds to fund capital expenditure and for general working capital. (published 0318 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDONESIA DELAYS GLOBAL SUKUK OFFER TO OCTOBER FROM JULY - OFFICIAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian government has delayed the planned offering of its first global sukuk to September or October from July or August as it is still seeking parliamentary approval for the offering, a senior official at the finance ministry told reporters Monday. (published 0337 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par JAPAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY LIKELY STEADY IF NODA BECOMES PM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda may have helped execute two dramatic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during his tenure, but that doesn't mean that should he become the next prime minister, his government would be more aggressively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PASSION LIES BENEATH BLAND EXTERIOR OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PM CANDIDATE NODA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yoshihiko Noda is hardly a household name in Japan, but that may be about to change dramatically. (published 0135 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: MALAYSIA INFLATION LIKELY ROSE 3.3% ON YEAR IN MAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's consumer price inflation likely continued to edge up in May, with the government's decision to reduce subsidies putting additional pressure on already high food and transport costs, economists said. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 20-06-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110620e76k000am\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
