\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Possible successors to Japan PM Kan\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2011\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 June 17 (Reuters) - Unpopular Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan defeated a no-confidence vote this month by pledging to quit once progress had been made in containing a nuclear crisis and recovering from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami that triggered it.\par \par \uc2 Now politicians are bickering over when Kan, already Japan's fifth premier in as many years, will actually resign.\par \par \uc2 Following are possible successors. All are lawmakers of the ruling Democratic Party of Japan.\par \par \uc2 YOSHIHIKO NODA\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Noda, 54, has backed Kan's push to limit new bond sales to cut huge public debt and fiscal reforms, including a proposed increase in the 5 percent sales tax to fund rising social security costs.\par \par \uc2 Last September he oversaw \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in six years to stem rapid yen gains. In March, at Noda's request, the Group of Seven conducted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 He drew fire from the opposition early in March after he said he had received a donation from a firm run by a man indicted for tax evasion. He said he would give the money back. This month he admitted to accepting donations from the founder of a group of cosmetic surgery hospitals being investigated over the death of a patient.\par \par \uc2 SEIJI MAEHARA\par \par \uc2 Maehara, 49, is a conservative defence policy expert who favours tight ties with the United States. Maehara quit as foreign minister in March after criticism for accepting donations from a foreign national. Taking political donations from foreign nationals is illegal if done intentionally. Maehara said he had done so unknowingly.\par \par \uc2 Maehara briefly led the Democratic Party in 2006. His hobbies include taking pictures of steam trains.\par \par \uc2 KAZUHIRO HARAGUCHI\par \par \uc2 A former internal affairs minister, Haraguchi, 51, is close to party power broker Ichiro Ozawa, a rival of Kan. He has criticised Kan's call for a higher sales tax to fund growing social security costs and curb public debt.\par \par \uc2 In a magazine article earlier this year, he threatened to create a "DPJ-A" group that would put priority on economic growth and stick to costly campaign promises.\par \par \uc2 BANRI KAIEDA\par \par \uc2 Kaieda, 62, is trade and energy minister, playing a key role in efforts to stabilise the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, which is still leaking radiation after being struck by the tsunami in March.\par \par \uc2 He has cautioned against raising the sales tax too soon, concerned about the impact on the economy. He has suggested he wants the Bank of Japan to do more to fight deflation, calling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discuss lifting a self-imposed ceiling on its government bond holdings.\par \par \uc2 YOSHITO SENGOKU\par \par \uc2 Sengoku, 65, was Kan's No.2 cabinet minister until January, when he was removed after an upper house censure motion over his handling of a territorial dispute with China.\par \par \uc2 The former lawyer has said that he feels a "sense of crisis" about Japan's public finances. Although a former member of the now defunct Socialist Party, he supports free-market policies.\par \par \uc2 YUKIO EDANO\par \par \uc2 Edano, 47, replaced Sengoku as chief cabinet secretary and for weeks was the public face of Japan during the crisis sparked by the March 11 disasters, impressing the public with his calm. Edano lacks clout within the party but polls show voters want him to play a greater political role.\par \par \uc2 MICHIHIKO KANO\par \par \uc2 Agriculture Minister Kano, 69, has led efforts to send aid to northern Japan's farming and fishing industries since the March disaster. He is cool to Japan joining the U.S.-led free trade pact TPP, saying rebuilding domestic industries should be the priority.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Linda Sieg; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/SUCCESSORS (FACTBOX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110620e76h000av\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
