\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Five facts about possible Japan PM candidate Noda\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2011\par 05:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 June 9 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, an advocate of fiscal discipline who backs a rise in sales tax to contain mounting debt, has emerged as a frontrunner to replace Naoto Kan as prime minister, a newspaper said on Thursday. [ID:nL3E7H83MN]\par \par \uc2 Here are key facts about Noda:\par \par \uc2 -- Noda, 54, took over from Kan as finance minister last June and has been in charge of budget, tax and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. He has backed Kan's push to limit new bond sales to cut huge public debt and fiscal reforms, including a proposed increase in the 5 percent sales tax to fund rising social security costs.\par \par \uc2 - Last September he oversaw \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in six years to stem rapid yen gains. In March, at Noda's request, the Group of Seven conducted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda shares Kan's view that fighting deflation is a policy priority but has been careful to avoid being seen as exerting pressure on the Bank of Japan, unlike some ruling party lawmakers who have called on the BOJ to adopt inflation targeting. More recently, he dismissed the idea floated by some lawmakers that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should buy special government bonds issued to fund post-quake reconstruction, effectively printing money.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda ran in a party leadership race in 2002 but lost to three senior lawmakers. His campaign platform included speedy structural reforms and bringing competition into the public sector.\par \par \uc2 -- Unlike many politicians, Noda is not from a privileged background. A son of a member of the military, he started in regional politics in 1987 and joined the Democratic Party about a decade ago when it was still in the opposition, having earned a reputation as an orator. A fan of combat sports, he is a keen judo practitioner.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Chisa Fujioka; Editing by Alex Richardson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/NODA (FACTBOX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110612e76900022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
