\par\par \b \uc2 DJ NY Fed: US Committed $1 Billion To March Effort To Weaken Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2011\par 18:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 DJ NY Fed: US Committed $1 Billion To March Effort To Weaken Yen By Michael S. Derby Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The U.S. government committed $1 billion to an effort in March to help arrest the appreciation of the Japanese yen in the wake of natural disaster that struck that nation.\par \par \uc2 In a report Friday, the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  said that it committed $500 billion from its System Open Market Account, while it acted as the agent for the Treasury Department's $500 billion contribution, which was drawn from the Exchange Stabilization Fund. The report said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred only once.\par \par \uc2 The New York Fed report said "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was consistent with the G-7 statement" which had explained participating governments were aiming to counter "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates." In the wake of the March 11 earthquake and its aftermath, the yen underwent a significant rise relative to other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and authorities worried the gains would complicate Japan's economic recovery prospects.\par \par \uc2 The report noted the Bank of Japan sold yen to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and added "much of the yen's depreciation associated with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place in the period immediately following the release of the G-7 statement."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was done in conjunction with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank of England, \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The report left undefined what U.S. authorities bought as they worked to weaken the yen. The number reported by the New York Fed was in line with what market participants had reckoned at the time.\par \par \uc2 The New York Fed said that in the quarter ended March 31, the dollar fell 5.5% against the euro and rose 2.5% against the yen. For the same period, the New York Fed's holdings of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated assets stood at $26.21 billion, while the Treasury's Exchange Stabilization Fund held $26.19 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported attributed the dollar's decline relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter to "a perceived weakening in the U.S. economic growth outlook relative to those of other countries." It also said rising oil prices, which raised further questions about future growth, hurt the dollar. Expectations of tighter monetary policy from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped erode the greenback, the New York Fed said. -By Michael S. Derby, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2214; michael.derby@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110514e75d00aku\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
