\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Looks Unlikely\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2011\par 19:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Even as the yen continues to flirt with levels against the dollar that spark talk of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to damp its rise, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems likely to hold its fire.\par \par \uc2 On March 18, when the world's attention and sympathy were focused on the earthquake-stricken country, the Japanese government, backed by central banks from the Group of Seven, sold yen and bought dollars in a bid to reverse the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's damaging ascent.\par \par \uc2 This time, though, the decision is less clear-cut. Most importantly, the dollar has fallen against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks as U.S. interest rates get dragged down by fears that the economy's momentum is slowing. Were Japan to seek to limit the yen's gains, it likely would be on its own, making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less likely to be successful.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't rule it out completely, but circumstances have changed," says Gareth Berry, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Singapore. "You could argue this is a different problem. This is about dollar weakness rather than yen strength."\par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed against the yen Monday, trading at Y80.71 midmorning in New York, compared with Y80.57 late Friday in New York. Late last week, the dollar dipped below Y80, a level that traders suggested could prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to initiate another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This level is considered important because many of the country's top employers, including auto and electronics exporters, make business-planning assumptions that the dollar won't fall below that level.\par \par \uc2 Other factors have changed in the seven weeks since the Group of Seven industrialized nations banded together to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the dollar is just back above Y80, the yen remains weaker against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including against those of its biggest trade partners, such as the euro zone, China, South Korea and Taiwan. Measured against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket of its trade partners, the yen is almost 5% weaker than it was before the March 18 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can make a country's goods more competitive globally and at home.\par \par \uc2 Another factor is the price of oil, which, even after last week's plunge, remains substantially higher than it was six months ago. A stronger yen benefits Japan by giving it more buying power for oil, which is priced in dollars. Japan imports the vast majority of its energy needs.\par \par \uc2 Government officials seem to have tipped that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't imminent. On Thursday, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said that "market conditions are different from those in March." On Friday, he said that the government will "closely monitor" the situation. Also on Friday, Economy Minister Koru Yosano described the situation as "not of the yen's unequaled strength but the dollar's weakness."\par \par \uc2 "The comments said in coded language 'We are OK with the dollar move so far'," said Adarsh Sinha, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  in Hong Kong, who added that he was surprised at how explicit the comments were.\par \par \uc2 Should Japan decide to re-enter the markets, it would likely have to go it alone, rather than rely on other G-7 members. Unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often seen by economists as less successful over the longer term. And they will require Japan to use more monetary firepower to move markets than if they had partners at the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Another deterrent: \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to work best when macroeconomic forces are already pointing in the direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the weeks after the March \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was strengthening on hopeful U.S. economic prospects and higher U.S. bond yields And the world was at its most uncertain and pessimistic about Japan during the worst of the twin natural and nuclear disasters. The dollar rose to as high as Y85.52 in early April.\par \par \uc2 Then things changed. U.S. bond yields starting to fall amid fears of a slowdown in the U.S. recovery. The dollar-yen rate is highly correlated to differences in bond yields in the U.S. and Japan. The smaller the difference, the less incentive that investors, especially those in Japan, have to buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be difficult to fight a broad dollar move that's happening for a lot of reasons that are not yen-specific. If U.S. yields keep moving lower, it will be very difficult to fight the trend," says Sinha of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So far, investors in sufficient quantities haven't pounced to drive the yen even higher against the dollar. Some are still fearful that, despite the government official's soft language, an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could emerge and wipe out their bets. Sinha pointed to domestic retail investors who have been buying dollars in recent days and who might have seen Y80 as a buying opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Many investors are of the view that the yen will weaken again, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not. The dollar "could rebound to Y85 without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we believe current levels provide a good opportunity to buy," \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at Nomura said in a note to clients Friday.\par \par \uc2 -By Alex Frangos, The Wall Street Journal; alex.frangos@wsj.com [ 05-09-11 1326ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110509e759000ex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
