\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Nervous Tone Prevails Ahead Of US Payrolls\b0\par\par\uc2 1,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2011\par 13:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets stable, nervous tone ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls\par \par \uc2 -- Euro remains depressed following cautious ECB\par \par \uc2 -- Zloty sharply stronger after Polish state-owned BGK bank sold euros\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets were relatively stable in European trading hours Friday with the dollar holding on to sharp overnight gains as a nervous tone prevailed after commodity prices tumbled and concerns over growth resurfaced.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With key U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due for release at 1230 GMT, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets treaded water during European hours with the dollar holding at firmer levels in the aftermath of Thursday's huge drop in the euro and the shakeout in commodities-related \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "[The drop in risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] could just be a flush-out, but it could be more than that. The data is looking soggy, and even the China data has been moderating--not alarmingly--but it seems that the tightening is working and if you put it all together it raises eyebrows," said Paul Mackel, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "People are getting nervous."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The initial trigger for this pullback came from a drop in the price of silver, which tumbled 8% Thursday, the biggest one-day percentage drop for the metal since Dec. 1, 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That dragged down \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  linked to commodities exports, spreading to oil-related \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as the commodities drop extended.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An exception to the sell-off was the Australian dollar, which was stronger against the greenback after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  indicated its growing concerns about inflation and set the stage for a rise in interest rates sooner than many had been expecting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The RBA has today [Friday] signaled via its underlying inflation forecasts that a rate hike is imminent. As such we have brought forward our forecast for the next rate hike from July to June," said Warren Higan, chief economist at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1145 GMT, the Australian dollar was changing hands at US$1.0677, up from US$1.05853 late Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. nonfarm payroll figures represent the next test for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones forecast a rise of 185,000 compared with a 216,000 gain in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the current environment a [nonfarm] payrolls result above 200k might easily cause a continuation of yesterday's [Thursday's] dollar recovery," said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But even 200,000 new jobs or a little more wouldn't be sufficient to shift the Federal Reserve's monetary policy stance towards normalization, the bank said. "The difference between the current unemployment rate [8.8%] and the Fed target of well below 6% is too notable for that to be the case," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro remains depressed against the dollar after shedding some four cents Thursday when European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet delivered a surprisingly cautious tone at his monthly press conference, and pointedly avoided signaling an imminent rate rise by not using the phrase that "strong vigilance" was needed against inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Trichet disappointed yesterday by not using the code word 'vigilant,'" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note to clients. "But we still expect the ECB to hike rates in July. Therefore this is a postponement of the next hike rather than a shift in policy and we see limited euro downside against the dollar from here."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was stable against the yen in Europe even though Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda offered a bit of a green light to dollar selling by suggesting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was in no rush to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again in the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants aren't ruling out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's rise against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Japanese economy is still very weak, they are still counting the cost of the earthquake so the last thing they really need is an excessively strong yen," said Daragh Maher, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Credit Agricole in London. "I wouldn't immediately dismiss [\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ]."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1152 GMT, the dollar was at Y80.240 against the yen, compared with a low of Y79.57 Thursday, the lowest since a round of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  March 18.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In emerging European markets, the Belarussian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  widened its official trading corridor for its ruble against a basket of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Belarussian ruble is now allowed to fluctuate up to 12% from a target basket made up of dollars, euros and the Russian ruble, compared with 8% earlier. Economists expect a larger devaluation to be a prerequisite for a rescue package of $3 billion from Moscow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The zloty was sharply stronger against the euro after Polish state-owned BGK bank sold euros, traders told Dow Jones Newswires. The amounts of the euro that BGK has sold remain unclear, they said. At 11.53 GMT the zloty was trading at PLN3.9478 against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1153 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.4528 against the dollar, compared with $1.4532 late Thursday in New York, according to trading system EBS. The dollar was at Y80.24 against the yen, compared with Y80.05, while the euro was at Y116.55 compared with Y116.42. The pound was trading at $1.64222 against the dollar, compared with $1.6489 late Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.104 compared with 74.086 late Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A summary of key levels for chart-watching technical strategists is below:\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/USD    USD/JPY    GBP/USD    USD/CHF \par  \par Spot 1044 GMT     1.4534     80.27      1.6414     0.8726 \par 3 Day Trend       Bearish    Bearish    Bearish    Bullish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish    Bearish    Bullish    Bearish \par 200 day ma        1.3759     83.75