\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Despite Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears, Market Is Different From March -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2011\par 03:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Yen's latest rise different from March surge\par \par \uc2 -Market volatility lower now\par \par \uc2 -Recent movement result of broad dollar weakness\par \par \uc2 By Stephen L. Bernard Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The yen's latest rise has put traders on high alert for a repeat of March's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks to weaken it.\par \par \uc2 But the circumstances this time are very different and that, market analysts say, makes it risky to predict an encore.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say there are two key differences between when the Group of Seven embarked on a historic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March after the dollar fell below Y80 and what is happening in the market now: Market volatility is much lower now than it was in March, and the yen's strength is mostly the result of a broad decline in the dollar and not a reflection of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 The dollar decline is what Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda attributed the yen's strength to during a speech Thursday as he tried to downplay the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell below Y80 Thursday for the first time since the G7's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 18 to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dropping as low at Y79.57, according to EBS via CQG. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was executed, in part, because market volatility rose sharply in the days before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading to wild swings in the yen.\par \par \uc2 'Implied volatility on the eve of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  [in March] was 17%. Today it's 11.7%,' said Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York. 'Volatility is not extreme. Movement is orderly.'\par \par \uc2 That key difference is why traders shouldn't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just because the dollar tumbled back below Y80, Chandler said. The dollar's move lower has been steadier than it was in March when the dollar fell at dizzying speed to record lows in the space of a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 'The risk is still fairly low,' Chandler said of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If implied volatility, a measure of expected volatility over the next three months, starts to surge again that would likely be a bigger trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  than hitting any specific price levels, he added.\par \par \uc2 A jump in volatility would imply this is more of a yen-focused move than one tied to the dollar, raising the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  being effective. The dollar has been falling broadly over the past month as some traders shun the greenback for higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while others look for safe havens to avoid ongoing geopolitical risk.\par \par \uc2 A price trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also less of an issue now because the dollar's record low against the yen is Y76.25, which it touched just before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March. Two months ago, the record low was at Y79.75, so then, dropping below Y80 was a historic move.\par \par \uc2 Market participants had also set a plethora of automatic sell orders around the previous all-time record, intending to minimize losses. Those automatic orders are no longer clustered around exactly one point in the market, but instead spread out over a wider band of prices, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 'Because of the March 17 movement, the market is not on a knife edge like before,' said Jeffrey Young, head of North American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York. That allows Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be more patient, Young added.\par \par \uc2 Even with no target levels necessarily in play as magic numbers to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say the lower the dollar falls against the yen, the higher the likelihood \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect its exporters.\par \par \uc2 'If it continues to drift down, the economy sputters and Japanese asset prices perform poorly, [\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again,' Young said.\par \par \uc2 -By Stephen L. Bernard, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4528; stephen.bernard@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110507e756000ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
