\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rallies As Investors Shun Risk, Euro Drops-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erin McCarthy Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 2011\par 23:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Global economic slowdown jitters and a broad commodity selloff Thursday combined to give the long-shunned dollar a dramatic boost.\par \par \uc2 The ICE dollar index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was on pace for its biggest daily gain in percentage terms since November. The euro dropped about 2% on the day against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falling victim to surprisingly dovish comments from the head of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the broader risk-off move.\par \par \uc2 An unexpected rise in U.S. jobless claims joined the week's string of softer global economic data, heightening concerns about a pullback in the global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar strength reflects the fact that investors sold a lot of dollars when they were optimistic on risk. As they pull back [on risk], they have to buy back those dollars," said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . "It reflects the fact that the U.S. economic data really deteriorated."\par \par \uc2 As a result, market participants pared back positions on recently high-flying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the euro, and commodities, which are priced in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and essentially are anti-dollar positions. Crude oil closed below $100 per barrel in New York, while silver futures fell 8%, giving the dollar added support.\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing a real barn-burning profit-taking," said Carl Forcheski, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But against the safe-harbor Japanese yen, the dollar slipped below Y80 for the first time since the Group of Seven's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 18.\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.4541 from $1.4827 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.05 from Y80.62, while the euro was at Y116.43 from Y119.47. The U.K. pound was at $1.6384 from $1.6489. The dollar was at CHF0.8702 from CHF0.8616.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 74.086 from about 73.031.\par \par \uc2 Investors were especially cautious ahead of Friday's U.S. nonfarm payrolls data, since a downside surprise could further escalate worries about a global economic slowdown, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, less-hawkish commentary from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, diminished market hopes for a June rate hike triggered the euro's initial dip and a reversal of short-dollar positions. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on track for its largest daily drop in percentage terms against the dollar since August 2010, according to Mark McCormick, strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also crushed the Australian and New Zealand dollars, whose fall tracked tumbling commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen, meanwhile, outperformed on the back of safe-haven bids. The dollar's dip below Y80 brought the prospects of a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  back into focus, but analysts say a less volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market lowers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's general recovery may not last, however, as the Federal Reserve is still likely to lag behind most of the world's central banks in monetary tightening, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "This is still a dollar downtrend. The Fed is still going to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is most likely going to be the most accommodative, other than the [Bank of Japan], over the next year," said McCormick. -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110506e75500pdx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
