\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,947 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2011\par 01:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    80.00-80.69      79.57-81.28 \par EUR/USD   1.4510-1.4657    1.4494-1.4755 \par AUD/USD   1.0532-1.0664    1.0512-1.0770 \par NZD/USD   0.7810-0.7888    0.7743-0.7940 \par GBP/USD   1.6357-1.6475    1.6306-1.6543 \par USD/CHF   0.8650-0.8713    0.8554-0.8748 \par USD/CAD   0.9626-0.9711    0.9564-0.9721 \par EUR/JPY   116.15-118.50    114.91-119.77 \par EUR/GBP   0.8852-0.8938    0.8842-0.9045 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate after hitting 7-week low of 79.57 yesterday as markets await 1230 GMT U.S. April non-farm payrolls data (forecast +185,000 vs +216,000 last) & unemployment rate (forecast is for no change at 8.8%). USD/JPY undermined by unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades amid by negative global risk sentiment (VIX fear gauge rose 6.56% to 18.2), nearly-10% plunge in oil prices and sharp Wall Street losses overnight (DJIA down 1.1%) on surprise 43,000 rise in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims (vs forecast for 19,000 fall) which amplified worries about economic outlook and today's employment report. USD/JPY also weighed by ultra-loose U.S. monetary policy, lower U.S. Treasury yields, concerns about massive U.S. fiscal imbalance, Japan exporter sales. But USD/JPY losses tempered by USD demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  import settlements; expectation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; positions adjustment before weekend. Other data: 2350 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  April monetary base, 1130 GMT Fed's Yellen speaks, 1400 GMT Fed's Dudley speaks, 1900 GMT U.S. March consumer credit. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold. Support at 80.00 (hourly chart), then at 79.57 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 78.44 (76.4% Fibonacci correction of 76.25-85.53 March 17-April 6 advance), then 76.25 (record low set March 17). Resistance at 80.69 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 81.19-81.28 band (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high), then 81.69 (Monday's high), 82.28 (April 28 high) and 82.82 (April 27 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias after ECB yesterday kept its policy rate at 1.25% as widely anticipated but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Trichet's comments were less hawkish than expected, giving no signal whether interest rates would be raised in June. EUR/USD also undermined by surprise 4% drop in German April manufacturing (vs forecast for no change), increased investor risk aversion, lingering worries over euro-zone sovereign debt woes. But EUR/USD losses tempered by ultra-loose U.S. monetary policy; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 1000 GMT German March industrial production index. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staged bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 1.4574 yesterday. Support at 1.4510 (yesterday's low), then at 1.4494 (April 26 low); breach would expose downside to 1.4380 (uptrend support line from Feb. 14 low of 1.3428), then 1.4222 (55-day moving average) and 1.4155 (April 18 reaction low). Resistance at 1.4657 (hourly 