h), 0.8062 (Tuesday's high) and 0.8119 (Monday's 3-year high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 2-week low of 1.6357 yesterday. BOE yesterday held its policy rate at its all-time low of 0.5% and its bond purchases unchanged at GBP200 billion as widely expected. GBP/USD undermined by higher investor risk aversion, bigger-than-expected drop in UK April services PMI to 54.3 in April from 57.1 in March (vs 55.5 forecast); fears of UK stagflation; concerns about adverse impact of UK government spending cuts and tax hikes on economy. But GBP/USD losses tempered by ultra-loose U.S. monetary policy; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0830 GMT UK April producer prices. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish; 5-day moving average staging bearish crossover against 15-day MA. Support at 1.6357 (yesterday's low); breach would target 1.6306 (April 20 low), then 1.6275 (55-day moving average), 1.6244 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of 1.5934-1.6745 March 28-April 28 advance) and 1.6229 (April 19 low). Resistance at 1.6475 (hourly chart), then at 1.6543 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6574 (Wednesday's high), then 1.6660 (Tuesday's high), 1.6737-1.6745 band (Monday's high-April 28 high) and 1.6877 (Nov. 16, 2009 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by broadly firmer USD undertone; but topside limited by unwinding of CHF-funded carry trades amid increased investor risk aversion; ultra-loose U.S. monetary policy; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0545 GMT Swiss April unemployment. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.8692 yesterday; negative MACD histogram bars contracting, pointing to increasing upside risks. Resistance at 0.8713 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.8748 (April 29 high), then 0.8760 (April 28 high) and 0.8835 (April 27 high). Support 