\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Down;3-Year Yield Likely Capped At 3.82%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2011\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds and bond futures inch lower as continued foreign selling in bond futures weigh amid a lack of other decisive leads, say analysts; the three-year yield is quoted up 2 bps at 3.80%, the five-year yield also up 2 bps at 4.14%, while the ten-year yield is up 2 bps at 4.50%. June bond futures are down 6 ticks at 102.86, as foreigners sell 6,344 contracts in net so far today. \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  analyst Yoon Yeo-sam tips a 3.75%-3.82% range for the three-year bond today and a 3.70%-4.00% band for next week, with the market's focus on the FOMC meeting outcome, as well as South Korea's monthly industrial output data. He expects Fed Chief Bernanke to make more "hawkish" remarks this time, likely saying that the latest round of quantitative easing will end as scheduled, with no further quantitative easing planned. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall from current 81.85 as some investors may cut their USD-long positions before going into the Easter weekend as there's some risk of China letting the CNY appreciate against the USD to tame the nation's inflation, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  senior dealer Tsutomu Soma. A weaker USD/CNY would likely weigh the USD against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as spur concerns China's economic growth engine may slow. "It's scary, at least for me, to leave the office for the weekend without squaring USD-long positions," he says, adding it's possible the pair may fall below 81.00 in the near term. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mixed; the Nikkei is flat, the Kospi is flat, the Taiex is up 0.1%, the Shanghai Composite is down 0.5%, the SET is down 0.6%, and the KLCI is down 0.1%. Markets in Australia, New Zealand, Hong Kong, India, Indonesia, the Philippines and Singapore are shut to mark Good Friday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD was little-changed as many investors shied away from active trading before the Easter holiday. The EUR/USD is at 1.4583 from 1.4553 in NY late Thursday, the EUR/JPY is at 119.38 from 119.16, and the USD/JPY is at 81.86 from 81.84. The Japanese government on Friday approved a Y4 trillion extra budget that involves no increase in bond issuance to finance quake-relief steps. Korean finance minister Yoon Jeung-hyun says foreign capital is flowing into local financial markets rapidly, adding that South Korea needs to strengthen monitoring of capital flows. Spot gold is at $1507.20/oz, up $2.50 from the NY close. The market for Nymex crude oil futures was shut after the June contract settled up 84 cents at $112.29/bbl in New York Thursday. (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end up 0.1% at 8969.43, closing at a fresh two-month high, on moderate volume of NT$113.77 billion, led by bank stocks and bargain-hunting in PC makers despite the shipment outlook remaining dismal; the Taiex has gained 3.8% since Wednesday. Rita Li, an analyst at Taiwan International Securities, says the Taiex faces tremendous resistance at 9000; "unless unexpectedly good news comes from the tech sector, the Taiex will probably remain choppy around current levels next week." She tips the index in a 8800-9200 range next week. \uc2 Mega Financial\uc2  (2886.TW) ended 1.9% higher at NT$24.35, Fubon Financial (2881.TW) rose 1.2% to NT$40.90, Acer (2353.TW) ended up 3.2% at NT$51.10, and \uc2 Compal Electronics\uc2  (2324.TW) closed 2.0% higher at NT$30.90. Apple's (AAPL) part suppliers are mixed: Hon Hai (2317.TW) is flat at NT$109.50, Largan (3008.TW) ended up 0.5% at NT$926.00, and Genius (3406.TW) rose 0.7% to NT$301.00. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0555 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 June futures are up 0.1% at 9690, in line with the cash market's advance, as investors begin to shift focus to a heavy slate of Japanese corporate earnings reports, as well as U.S. macroeconomic indicators due out next week. After the Easter holiday is over, comments from the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee and Fed Chairman Bernanke will be two highlights to watch for, says Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities. In terms of macro data, the S&P/Case-Shiller Home Price Indices and U.S. GDP numbers will be the most watched, he says. Hirano pegs next week's futures range at 9500-9800. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is off its earlier 1,082.80 high on a bout of fund-inflows related to foreign buying of local stocks; the pair is at 1,080.70 from 1,080.30 late Thursday in Seoul, traders say. "The pair may slip marginally below the 1,080 mark" before the end of local trade, but the authorities aren't likely to let the pair fall too much from current levels, not after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite actively in support of the USD to support the pair around 1,080 Thursday, says a local bank trader. Traders tip initial support at 1,080 and then at 1,078. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares fall 0.6% midday to 1103.32 as local players take profit after five straight days of gains. Volume is light in the absence of many offshore players as many overseas markets are closed for Good Friday. "The market is just experiencing a correction, at the time when most foreign buyers are not around," says Bualuang Securities strategist Pongrat Ratanatawanananda; but she adds, continued capital inflows amid renewed risk appetite will likely continue to bolster stock market sentiment in the medium-term, while support at 1100 is likely to hold for the day with resistance pegged at 1110. Among most actives, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is down 1.0% at THB766, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is up 0.5% at THB56.25, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) is down 0.6% at THB176.50, and PTT (PTT.TH) falls 1.0% to THB379. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0507 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese investors are likely to continue buying JGBs in coming sessions ahead of the country's Golden Week holidays on April 29 and May 3-5, as a respite from auctions in the long- and super-long end keeps supply-side concerns at bay, says Makoto Yamashita, chief Japan interest rate strategist at Deutsche Securities. "We think investors will have little choice but to buy with the Golden Week holiday approaching and considering that no auctions are scheduled for the superlong and long-term zones until the 10-year auction on May 12." Yamashita recommends buying the benchmark 10-year JGB "on expectations that the yield will fall below 1.2%;" the yield is currently down 1.5 bps at 1.210%. He says economic weakness after last month's disaster, as well as higher oil prices, should support safe-haven JGB demand. Still, he says attention on political discussions concerning a possible reconstruction tax is needed after the Golden Week holidays. If such a tax doesn't find traction, issuance concerns could flare, hurting JGBs, analysts say.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall next week, at a pace fast enough to prompt investors to start speculating \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF might step in with JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  senior dealer Satoshi Tate. The pair is at 81.88; it may fall as low as 80.00 after Tuesday-Wednesday FOMC meeting, he says. Judging from the latest U.S. economic conditions and recent remarks by Fed officials, Bernanke will sound dovish enough to disappoint USD-bullish investors, he says. "At the press conference, investors will find out the FOMC is not leaning toward policy tightening and there won't be a good chance of such an action for the rest of this year," he says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is down 0.1% at 1524.48 midday in lackluster trade. The index was almost flat all morning and stayed within a tight 5-point range, and is likely to continue to move sideways in the afternoon session in the absence of leads, a dealer says. "Market activity is dominated by lower liners...we have some retail buyers picking up cheap stocks but the heavyweights are flat," the dealer says. He tips a 1520-1530 range for the rest of the day. Market breadth is negative with 351 decliners to 280 advancers. Among decliners, Genting (3182.KU) is down 0.9% at MYR11.48, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) is down 0.7% at MYR5.36 and Maybulk (5077.KU) is down 1.9% at MYR2.60. Among gainers, \uc2 JT International\uc2  (2615.KU) is up 2.4% at MYR7.17 and Naim (5073.KU) is up 1.4% at MYR2.95. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0439 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei moves into positive territory early in afternoon trading, now up 0.2% at 9706.63 after climbing as high as 9726.97. Yoshihiro Okumura, general manager of research at Chibagin Asset Management, notes that automakers are leading the market. \uc2 Renesas Electronics\uc2  (6723.TO) said it now expects its chip-making plant in Ibaraki Prefecture (north of Tokyo), currently suspended due to the March 11 earthquake, will partly resume production on June 15, earlier than the previous forecast July restart. Renesas (a major global supplier of chips used in cars) is up 2.1% at Y723, rising sharply on the news of the plant restart. "Investors may be hopeful that Toyota is going to announce positive news on its plant production plans," says Okumura. Toyota President Akio Toyoda is set to brief on the company's production plans at 0600 GMT. 21/33 Topix subindexes are up, with Toyota up 1.6% Y3,250, and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.3% at Y3,115. Okumura pegs the afternoon Nikkei range at 9600-9750, noting that the stabilizing yen at the high-81 level may also be providing relief for stocks. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 22 Apr 2011 02:33 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Down;3-Year Yield Likely -2-\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 0.8% at 456.45, up slightly from an intraday low of 454, with local investors selling on inflation concerns after major cities earlier this week forecasted CPI rose by more than 3.0% in April, dealers say. "Sentiment is low because most institutional investors are staying on the sidelines or moving funds into banks which are offering an interest rate of 14%, because the government continues to emphasize cutting public spending and anti-inflation in the second quarter," says a trader with EuroCapital Securities. He added share prices are likely to fall next week if the government allows state-run traders to raise fuel prices after the recent rise in global oil prices. Volume was thin at 23.6 million shares, valued at VND449 billion. Among the most active stocks, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ends down 2.0% at VND19,300, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) closed 0.7% lower at VND13,400 and Eximbank (EIB.VH) finished flat at VND14,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 22, 2011 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110422e74m000nd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
