\par\par \b \uc2 Sumitomo Life Exec: Dollar/Yen May Dip Below Y80 Again In Jan-Mar 2012\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2011\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A senior executive with \uc2 Sumitomo Life Insurance Co\uc2 . said he expects the yen's strength to continue into next year, with the dollar again falling below Y80 by the January-March quarter of 2012.\par \par \uc2 "Although (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, I don't think the yen's appreciation has ended," \uc2 Michihisa Tanimoto\uc2 , managing director and director of the board at Japan's fourth largest life insurer by assets, told Dow Jones Newswires in a recent interview. "I expect the dollar/yen to touch below Y80 one more time from around the end of this year to the beginning of next year--probably in the January-March period," he said.\par \par \uc2 But Tanimoto said he thinks that would be the "final round" of yen appreciation, with the pair to recover toward Y120 within a few years. Improvements in the U.S. economy and the Federal Reserve's exit from easy monetary policy--leading to a widening gap between Japan and the U.S. interest rates--would be the major premises of such a move, he said.\par \par \uc2 After the Group of Seven industrialized countries jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's devastating March 11 earthquake, the yen slid into a downward trend against the dollar. It has since moved higher amid a general sell-off in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sumitomo Life forecasts the dollar will trade in a Y78-Y95 range and the euro in a Y100-Y130 band for the current fiscal year that started April 1. As of 0630 GMT, the dollar was at Y82.00 and the euro was at Y119.83.\par \par \uc2 Tanimoto doesn't expect any "drastic rises in yields," despite expectations the government will issue more debt to pay for restoration of areas damaged by the earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 "Yields may rise due to speculation about reconstruction bonds, but it would only be temporary," he said. "It is hard to expect sharp rises in interest rates unless the nation gets out of this deflationary environment."\par \par \uc2 The life insurer expects the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield to keep a 0.8%-1.6% range for this business year, while the 20-year yield is expected to move in a 1.9%-2.4% band. As of 0630 GMT, the 10-year JGB yield was at 1.23%, and the 20-year yield stood at 2.00%.\par \par \uc2 It also sees the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield in a 2.75%-4.50% band, compared with the current 3.41%.\par \par \uc2 The company plans to boost its domestic bond holdings--mainly superlong government bonds--by a couple of hundred billion yen during the fiscal year, following increases of around Y1 trillion in the previous year. Of Y20.633 trillion total assets in its general account, Y9.176 trillion, or 44.5%, were in yen-denominated bonds at the end of December.\par \par \uc2 -By Megumi Fujikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com [ 21-04-11 0700GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110421e74l000k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
