\par\par \b \uc2 ADB Economist Rhee: High Inflation Pressure Is Building Up In Developing Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2011\par 08:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A senior official at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on March 18 by the Group of Seven leading nations was "very effective" in taming a sharp rise in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Takehiko Nakao, director general of the ministry's international bureau, also said at a seminar in Tokyo that Japan should move quickly to tackle its huge national debt, which stands at around double its annual economic output.\par \par \uc2 The G-7---made up of the U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada---jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in a decade on March 18 to curb what they saw as excessive strength in the yen. The joint action came after the dollar hit a record low of Y76.25 on March 17 in the wake of the earthquake and tsunami that struck the nation on March 11.\par \par \uc2 Neither Japan nor the group has stepped into the market since. In the past weeks, the greenback has stayed above Y82. At 0845 GMT, the dollar was at Y82.87.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Nakao said the cost for reconstruction after the March 11 disaster could be larger than that for the 1995 Kobe earthquake, but that the expenditure for the latest disaster is "not permanent".\par \par \uc2 He also quoted Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's as saying that "fiscal consolidation will still be an important goal of Japanese government policy."\par \par \uc2 About 95% of Japanese government bonds are currently held by domestic investors, leaving little need to rely on overseas investors for financing. But Nakao said the government can't assume that Japanese investors will keep buying JGBs forever, adding that the country should address its worsening fiscal condition "sooner than later".\par \par \uc2 Nakao also referred to sovereign debt conditions in some European nations as "dire," as market yields on government bonds for countries like Greece, Ireland, and Portugal have remained high even after they asked for help with financing from the European Union and the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 "We should support European efforts to solve the issue," Nakao said.\par \par \uc2 -By Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-6269-2780; tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com [ 18-04-11 0955GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110418e74i000dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
