\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:Geithner: G-7  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In Yen Contained Risk Of Damaging Recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2011\par 16:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Noda comments and background starting in sixth paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--A Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen last month was successful, helping contain potential damage to Japan's fragile post-earthquake economy and the weak global recovery, the U.S. Treasury secretary said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, speaking after a bilateral meeting with Timothy Geithner, said the G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "very significant and effective."\par \par \uc2 Geithner said, "I think it is very important that we were able to come together as the G-7 in exceptional circumstances a few weeks ago a help Japan contain the risk that a sharp appreciation of the yen would damage the recovery."\par \par \uc2 "And it was a good sign of the world's support for Japan and the capacity of the major economies to come together ... and do something useful for the global economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 Neither official responded to a question about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seeking, or the G-7 would approve, another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Noda has said he would turn to other G-7 countries for help in coordinating another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the yen if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  became "excessively volatile." A Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Friday that Noda didn't discuss future joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Geithner.\par \par \uc2 Noda told The Wall Street Journal in an interview Friday that he would take "decisive steps" to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it becomes "excessively volatile," even while conceding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market had "calmed down" since March's concerted G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Noda's comments indicate that Japanese authorities remain vigilant on the yen even though they aren't too worried about its moderate rebound over recent trading sessions. The Japanese finance minister said Thursday he would request continued cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at a planned meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs and hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unilaterally if that becomes necessary because of sharp yen rises.\par \par \uc2 "Our fundamental stance is that if there is excessive volatility or disorderly movement, we will take decisive steps," Noda said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 After the bilateral meeting Friday, Noda thanked Geithner for responding to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's request for a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : "Secretary Geithner was so kind ... to quickly respond to our request."\par \par \uc2 "We wanted by all means to avoid the situation whereby the further appreciation of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would further negatively impact the Japanese economy," Noda said. "That situation was well appreciated by the G-7 countries ... the joint consultative \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  done in the market by the G-7 countries was significant and effective," he added.\par \par \uc2 Noda said Japan wants to emerge from the tragedy "as an even better and strong country and we would like to once again start to make a great contribution to the global economy."\par \par \uc2 Geithner said, "We feel this tragedy deeply as Americans," and that the U.S. has great admiration and confidence "in the basic strength and resilience and of the Japanese people and in the Japanese economy."\par \par \uc2 -By Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires, 202-631-5794; ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 04-15-11 1059ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110415e74f0009a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
