\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister: G-7 Agrees March 18 Joint  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Was Meaningful\b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2011\par 05:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Group of Seven officials agreed Friday that it is meaningful for the member nations to cooperate in combating 'disorderly movement' or 'excess volatility' in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Japan's finance minister said.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's comment followed a meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs held earlier in the day. The event has attracted attention from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders wondering whether the group would signal continued support to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fight against possible yen surges.\par \par \uc2 Joint G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted March 18 'was an attempt to deal with disorderly movement, excess volatility in the market,' Noda said. 'The measure was effective, as it has successfully curbed such conditions. And I believe that we agreed on the view (during the meeting) that it is meaningful for the G-7 to cooperate in that kind of situation.'\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110415e74f003xp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
