\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Fall To 83.00;China Data Eyed-\uc2 Barclays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2011\par 03:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may fall towards 83.00 if China inflation indicators due 0200 GMT turn out to be stronger than expected, raising concerns over potential tightening steps and an economic slowdown in China, leading to falls in share prices or U.S. Treasury yields, Barclays Bank chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Masafumi Yamamoto says in a note. A Dow Jones poll tips China March CPI likely rose 5.3% on year. Although the USD/JPY fell to a two-week low of 82.95 Thursday and Japan Finance Minister Noda said overnight that he will ask the G-7 to continue to cooperate on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "the likelihood of a coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is low at the moment," Yamamoto adds. Serious caution over potential unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't surface unless the USD/JPY falls toward 82.00 and JPY strength causes the Nikkei to fall below 9000, he says. The USD/JPY is at 83.72, off an earlier low of 83.47.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] Stockpiles of iron ore at Chinese steel mills and ports are running low, and the resulting supply tightness could drive prices for the commodity above $200/ton, analysts at Southern Cross Equities say. "Our contacts in China tell us the major steel mills in China made a costly mistake lower down sitting out of the market when the Japanese event hit, as they believed the price would come back sub-$150/ton as the major Japanese steel mills were down for a period of time." In fact, the market bottomed out almost simultaneously with the Tohoku earthquake: the recent trough for The Steel Index iron ore swaps at CME was at $163.13/ton CFR China on Mar. 14, the first trading day after the quake, since when it has risen to $178.98/ton last. Southern Cross says that mills have engaged in "solid buying" of iron ore in recent weeks and "you can easily see iron ore heading above $200 per ton near term". (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD's status as a proxy for less liquid emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia is a factor behind it's strength at the moment, but with time it will fade, says Sue Trinh, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC Capital; "this proxy status is another reason behind the AUD's ability to defy some of the higher frequency valuation metrics, such as interest rate differentials, which have recently suggested 'fairer' value for the AUD was lower." Trinh adds, the accelerated pace of progress toward CNY internationalisation will promote greater and greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and liquidity in Asian emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the years ahead, and this should see the AUD eventually lose its proxy status. Ultimately, Asian EMFX appreciation will no longer be considered unambiguously positive for AUD. The AUD/USD is now at 1.0537, just short of a 29-year high. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is off 0.1% at 3154.80, after a briefly positive open, with mixed cues from Wall Street and the upcoming release of China inflation and growth data at 0000 GMT keeping sentiment cautious. Thursday, U.S stocks were mixed after some disappointing weekly U.S. jobs data, while China's CPI numbers for March will be keenly watched for more signals on further tightening by Asia's growth powerhouse; a Dow Jones poll tips March CPI at +5.3% on year. Kim Eng says "expect the STI to trade in a tight range today despite numerous efforts to keep the bulls afloat above its strong support level around 3120." It tips immediate resistance at 3180, and notes the 14-day RSI is above its neutrality level at 60%. \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  (C31.SG) is up 0.6% at S$3.43 after it says its unit is buying a 40% stake in Surbana Corp., a unit of Singapore sovereign wealth fund \uc2 Temasek Holdings\uc2 , for S$360 million. Market volume is 242 million shares worth S$161 million. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is a tad lower at 8,660 from 8,665 late Thursday, but is likely to consolidate within a narrow range today as the pair is already oversold although underlying sentiment remains bullish on the IDR, dealers say. "There was good two-way interest around 8,659-8,661, and I expect the pair will remain trapped in a narrow range today," a dealer says; dealers tip 8,650-8,670 range for the day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are likely to move higher, possibly getting a boost from overnight gains in PLDT ADRs in New York and the rebound on Wall Street from intraday lows. The bellwether PSEi may test strong resistance at 4250 after closing 0.7% higher at 4234.96 on Thursday, with traders pegging imediate support at 4200 and next resistance at 4300. "PLDT advanced strongly last night in New York and that should help the market since it is accounts for a third of the index," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is 0.3% higher at 1530.67 on bargain buying in thin volume after Thursday's losses. A local dealer says sentiment is positive but tips the index to trade largely sideways and some profit taking to creep in later ahead of the weekend. "We reckon the key market barometer will probably continue to drift with a downward bias today," says HwangDBS. "Investors are likely to remain wary ahead of the Sarawak state elections Saturday... reflecting the cautious sentiment, trading interest is expected to be thin just like Thursday, which saw fewer than one billion shares changing hands," it says. The dealer says the index will likely trade in a 1515-1535 range today. Market breadth is positive with 151 advancers compared with 35 decliners. Among advancers, \uc2 YTL Corp\uc2  (4677.KU) is up 3.8% at MYR7.74, CIMB (1023.KU) is up 1.0% at MYR8.28 and Axiata (6888.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR4.79. Decliner \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  (3794.KU) is down 0.7% at MYR7.35. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds open flat despite negative cues from U.S. Treasurys as a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides some price support, an analyst at a local bank says. The USD/SGD is at 1.2464, after hitting a fresh low of 1.2453 Thursday following the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's move to tighten policy. "Despite the fact that equities are likely to rise and the negative cues from U.S. Treasurys, bonds may be a little sidelined today," she says, adding trade could track \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations as investors seek profits from a rising SGD. She tips a tight trading range, with yields unlikely to move much across the curve. Government bond yields were little changed Thursday; the two-year yield was flat at 0.43%, while the 10-year yield was unchanged at 2.43%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY may fall towards the 86.70-87.00 region if China's March CPI turns out to be stronger than expected, "strengthening expectations for additional tightening steps in China and prompting a sell-off in commodity-related shares in stock markets," Junichi Ishikawa, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IG Markets Securities in Japan, says in a note. He adds some caution has already surfaced over possible further China tightening after Hong Kong-based broadcaster Phoenix TV reported on Thursday that China's March CPI likely rose 5.3%-5.4% on year. A Dow Jones poll tips March CPI likely rose 5.3% on year. The AUD/JPY is last at 88.21, off an earlier low of 87.98. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude futures may have had their correction and benchmark contracts are expected resume their uptrend. Market participants say many traders are still very bullish on oil because the USD's outlook remains weak, while fighting in Libya and political tensions in its neighboring states persist. "We continue to believe that prices will advance again to post new highs before Memorial Day. The Fed is not going to just stop, cold turkey, once QE2 is over, and the dollar still looks weak here," says Cameron Hanover in a note. Meanwhile, Ritterbusch and Associates expects crude to hit fresh highs and tips Brent to eventually rise above $130; "Our expectations are still premised on a continued stalemate in Libya, a renewed weakening in the dollar and a need to maintain some fear premium related to protests across various MENA countries," it says. ICE June Brent crude is 49 cents higher at $122.49/bbl after the May contract expired overnight. Nymex May crude is up 52 cents at $108.63/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is near flat at 4880.3 in quiet trading before China's economic data due 0200 GMT, with CPI data likely closely monitored for monetary policy implications. "We are certainly waiting for Chinese economic data to come out and we had very little lead from overnight markets," says Macquarie Private Wealth Division director Martin Lakos. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) is down 0.9% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.6% after LME copper fell 1.0%, though Newcrest (NCM.AU) is up 2.2% after spot gold set a fresh record high of US$1,479.44/oz. Banks are also mixed, with \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 0.4% and NAB (NAB.AU) down 0.3%, as are consumer staples, with \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.5% after a strong sales outlook from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , and Woolworths (WOW.AU) down 0.3% after Macquarie downgraded the stock to Underperform. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI may open near flat ahead of key China economic data expected at 0200 GMT, including March CPI. HK-based broadcaster Phoenix TV reports Thursday that China's March CPI likely rose 5.3%-5.4% on year, following February's 4.9% gain, above the government's full-year target. This prompts expectations that "China is likely to increase banks' reserve requirement ratio further in the near term to curb inflation," according to Kenny Tang, general manager of AMTD Financial Planning. The HSI falls 0.5% to 24,014.00 Thursday, and market volume declined for the fourth consecutive session, to HK$71.84 billion, marking the lowest level in April so far and indicating heightened investor caution. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 04-14-11 2146ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110415e74f00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
