\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesian Shares Down 0.6%; 3710 Support Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 05:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are down 0.6% at 3723.732 in light volume, off an opening low of 3718.237, weighed by declines in most Asian markets amid concerns over the nuclear situation in Japan, traders say. They expect the main index to test 3710 support. "Bargain buying in select bank stocks on hopes that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will keep its key rate unchanged at 6.75% has lifted the main index from its lows," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Among decliners CPO producer London Sumatra (LSIP.JK) is down 2.0% at IDR2,375, tin producer Timah (TINS.JK) is down 1.7% at IDR2,850, while coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is down 0.8% at IDR3,200 on profit-taking. Bucking the trend Bank Danamon (BDMN.JK) is up 1.6% at IDR6,550. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is moving decisively down after Monday's record high, and is off $5.80, or 0.4%, at $1,457.40/oz, while silver is down eight cents at $40.14/oz. Gary Glover, senior client adviser at Novus Capital in Sydney, views the market as being deep in bubble territory but argues that it will likely run up further before it breaks: "Gold's been having a fairly orderly rally. It needs to accelerate into something a bit more vertical before we have a break, although silver looks pretty vertical at this stage," he says. He argues that this may come sooner rather than later: "I wouldn't want to be holding gold by the end of April." (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Korea looks set to raise its policy rate again in May, says Capital Economics economist Sukhy Ubhi, noting that the BOK forecasts that the economy is on track for solid growth, while price pressures have risen. Recent gains in the KRW have helped mitigate inflation, but Ubhi cautions that the BOK "will not want to rely to much on inherently volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." Ubhi also notes that the uptrend in producer price inflation suggests that core inflation has much further to climb. (alastair.gale@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is higher, buoyed by the USD's strength against the EUR and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair is at 43.25 from 43.08 late Monday; volume is moderate. The pair is also lifted by news Philippine export growth in February was the slowest since November 2009, says a local bank trader. He adds, the pair may stay within a 43.00-43.30 band for the session as participants closely track the EUR's movement. The Philippines' February exports rose 8.2% on-year to $3.86 billion. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is down 1.3% at 2095.18 as foreign investors snap their 18-session buying spree, selling KRW5.48 billion worth of shares. While the sales amount is small so far, "the won's weakness (against the U.S. dollar) will likely lead offshore short-term capital to sell more stocks for profit," says KTB Securities analyst Park Seok-hyun; "if inflation pressures become more serious, it could lead to more foreign fund outflows for the mid-to-long term." He tips the next support for the Kospi at 2070. Although the BOK's decision to keep rates steady has little impact on stocks, the market is keenly watching for the governor's post-meeting comments, especially on inflation, analysts say. Most heavyweight stocks are pressured by foreign selling; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) is off 0.9% at KRW886,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) is down 1.2% at KRW203,500 and \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) falls 2.1% to KRW465,500. But steelmaker Posco (005490.SE) gains 0.6% to KRW483,000 on bargain hunting after a four-session losing streak. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by one-month USD/JPY at-the-money options rises to 10.55%/11.25% from 10.45%/10.75% late Monday in New York "on growing uncertainty over how fast the dollar-yen could fall" as investors take profits from recent gains in the underlying exchange rate, says a senior options dealer at a major Japanese bank. Such uncertainty prompted some investors to buy short-dated USD-put/JPY-call options contracts, he says. The USD/JPY is at 83.95 from an earlier high of 84.78. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  raises its end-2011 SET index and SET 50 targets to 1150 and 835 from 1060 and 740, respectively. "We expect that further upward revisions in earnings will continue for banks through loan growth driven by corporate re-leveraging and rising interest rates, while Energy and Chemicals will see revisions from rising oil prices and volume growth," the house says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  maintains Overweight recommendations on the energy and bank sectors. The house removes Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) and Banpu (BANPU.TH) from its top pick list due to their recent performance and limited near-term earnings momentum relative to their peers and replaces them with PTT (PTT.TH) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Siam Commercial Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SCB.TH). Other top picks are \f28 \uc2 \f2 Krung Thai Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (KTB.TH), PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH), LPN Development (LPN.TH) and Ticon Industrial Connection (TICON.TH). (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0238 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is back on the rise as the Kospi extends its decline to lose 1.3%, while regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are broadly weak, weighing on the KRW, traders say; the pair is at 1,091.80 from 1,084.30 late Monday in Seoul. A local bank trader expects the pair to test the 1,093 mark later. "Offshore players who lapped up the dollar in early trade were seen selling at levels above 1,092, but so long as there's no active dollar-selling by exporters," the pair may be in for further gains as more short-USD covering may kick in, he says. He tips a tight 1,089-1,094 range for the next few hours. The KRW is largely brushing off BOK's decision to stand pat on rates, with eyes now on the BOK governor's post-meeting comments, traders say. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are expected to fall, tracking the weakness in the regional bourses. The three-day holiday starting from Wednesday is likely to prompt some investors to cash in their profits. Finansia Syrus Securities analyst Somchai Anektaweepon says falls may be trimmed late in the session on bargain hunting; he adds that the benchmark is biased upwards when the market reopens next week after the holiday as the fundamentals of the Thai market and Thai economy remain sound. He tips a 1068-1080 range for today; the SET Index ends down 0.6% at 1076.33 Monday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY could fall to 83.00-83.50 in the near term, says Citibank Japan chief strategist Osamu Takashima. "It's highly likely the pair has entered into a period of slippage, capped by the 5-day moving average, (which is now around 85.00,) given some technical overheating, before reaching the high hit after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last September" at 85.94, he says. Fed Vice Chair Yellen and NY Fed's Dudley didn't make any comments Monday about the Fed approaching the end of quantitative easing and that's weighing on the USD as well, Takashima says. The pair is at 84.24 from an earlier high of 84.78. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are a tad higher after the PBOC offers CNY140 billion worth of 28-day repos in an open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , its largest 28-day repo offering since January 2010. "The notable repo offering indicated the necessity of an imminent reserve requirement ratio hike to absorb liquidity is low," says a Shanghai-based analyst with a local insurer. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.75, up from Monday's close at 127.74, and is tipped to stay in a 127.72-127.80 range for the session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com) [ 04-11-11 2313ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110412e74c00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
