\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Could Fall To 83.00-83.50 Near-Term -Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY could fall to 83.00-83.50 in the near term, says Citibank Japan chief strategist Osamu Takashima. "It's highly likely the pair has entered into a period of slippage, capped by the 5-day moving average, (which is now around 85.00,) given some technical overheating, before reaching the high hit after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last September" at 85.94, he says. Fed Vice Chair Yellen and NY Fed's Dudley didn't make any comments Monday about the Fed approaching the end of quantitative easing and that's weighing on the USD as well, Takashima says. The pair is at 84.24 from an earlier high of 84.78. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are a tad higher after the PBOC offers CNY140 billion worth of 28-day repos in an open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its largest 28-day repo offering since January 2010. "The notable repo offering indicated the necessity of an imminent reserve requirement ratio hike to absorb liquidity is low," says a Shanghai-based analyst with a local insurer. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.75, up from Monday's close at 127.74, and is tipped to stay in a 127.72-127.80 range for the session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD retreats through the morning session with weakness in Asian shares. Greg Gibbs, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBS, says markets are reacting to news Japan's nuclear agency has upgraded the severity of the Fukushima crisis to the highest possible level. He adds AUD positioning was heavily long and so the pair was likely to see some profit-taking at some point. He also says it's a consolidation that was somewhat overdue. The AUD/USD is now at 1.0454, down from 1.0510 earlier. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] Whether the HSI's 1.4% fall to 23,973.96 marks the end of the current round of rally or the market is going through normal profit-taking before resuming the rally is difficult to call for now. The HSI's point and percentage drop for now are both the steepest after the market bottomed out at its year-to-date low of 22,123 on March 17. 37 of 45 blue chips are in negative territory, indicating broad-based profit-taking pressure. The HSI's intraday movement will be keenly eyed; if it can manage to recoup some of its early losses in the afternoon, then today's fall may be taken as normal profit-taking; during the HSI's recent rally, it's seldom the index will trend lower throughout the session. Market volume is not terribly heavy at HK$21.02 billion, and may be taken as an indication that selling pressure is not terribly heavy. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  is widely expected to lift its benchmark interest rate for a fourth-straight meeting on April 20 (the decision is expected around 0730 GMT), following a series of signals from authorities that its policy normalization efforts have yet to end as inflationary pressure builds. All 12 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires project a quarter percentage point hike to 2.75%. The BOT has lifted its key rate by a total of 125 bps since July last year. "The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  has clearly signalled that interest rates will remain on a gradual uptrend as inflation risks continue to outweigh growth risks," says Julia Goh, an economist with CIMB Investment Bank. "So far, domestic demand has continued to expand despite higher inflation expectations and borrowing costs." (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is down 1.6% at 9563.93 midday following a firmer yen (USD/JPY at 84.20), repeated aftershocks and renewed nuclear concerns. Japan's nuclear safety agency raised the crisis level of the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant accident from 5 to 7, the worst on the international scale. "Japanese exporters are already being hit by parts shortages and a lack of electricity so they're not ready for a firmer yen," says Cosmo Securities strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi, pegging afternoon support at 9500. 31/33 Topix subindexes are lower, with Inpex (1605.TO) off 4.1% at Y627,000, \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  (5020.TO) down 3.1% at Y527 on a drop in crude futures. \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) is off 3.3% at Y1,577 on a \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  downgrade to Equal-weight. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) is up 7.8% at Y539 in another volatile, high-volume session. Among exporters, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is off 3.0% at Y2,500. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are retreating as investors continue to book profit amid lingering inflation concerns. The benchmark PSEi is off 0.8% at 4193.65 in light volume, with losers pouncing on gainers, 61 to 17. "We won't be surprised if the market falls further considering what the market has gained since February. We'll be happy with any (PSEi) level above 4000," says Erwin Balita, reseach head at SB Equities. Immediate PSEi support is pegged at 4150, while resistance is at 4250. Losers are led by Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH), down 1.1% at PHP11.12, while San Miguel (SMC.PH) is down 5.6% at PHP151 as investors react to the lower-than-expected amount to be raised from the company's shares and bond offer compared to an earlier projection. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea bond futures are up four ticks at 102.94 in volatile trade after gaining as much as 18 ticks earlier in a knee-jerk response to the BOK's decision to keep rates on hold at 3.00%. Though foreign buying of 6,045 contracts in net so far today is providing support, caution has kicked in ahead of the BOK governor's post-meeting comments expected from around 0220 GMT, participants say. If he takes on an easy tone as he did during the March meeting bond futures will likely regain strength, but if he takes on a "hawkish" tone bonds are likely to pull back from their current levels, says a bond manager with a securities firm. He tips support for futures at 102.80. Local treasuries are mixed with the shorter ends outperforming the longer ends; the three-year yield is quoted down two bps at 3.76%, while the ten-year yield is up one bp at 4.50%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY trades higher after the PBOC sets the central parity at 6.5440, compared to 6.5401 Monday. The pair is now at 6.5450 from 6.5383 late Monday, and is tipped to trade around current levels for the rest of the session. A trader at a Shanghai-based foreign bank says the onshore USD is also tracking the dollar's strength in other Asian markets; the ICE Dollar Index is at 75.113 from 75.031 late Monday in New York. But she notes many traders may tend towards building up positions betting on yuan appreciation before China releases 1Q economic data Friday as the PBOC may let the yuan continue to rise to combat imported inflation. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is higher at 29.029 from 28.992 late Monday, as weak local shares (the Taiex is down 1.3%) prompt both foreign and local investors to accelerate buying in the greenback, says a local trader. "We saw some foreign outflows this morning, and some investors also started to cover their short positions when they saw the USD rise above NT$29 at the open." He notes some buying is also related to oil settlements. He reckons selling by exporters is likely to cap the pair at 29.100, while support is tipped at 28.930. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] All base metals on the LME are down with the exception of tin, which is yet to trade. Copper is off $30 at $9,825/ton, aluminum down $12.75 at $2,675.25/ton, and zinc the biggest decliner, down $29, or 1.1%, at $2,516/ton. A Hong Kong-based trader expects investors to take profits after the complex's frothy push close to recent records in the past week: "I think it just got a little bit overdone and with oil off overnight you're seeing people starting to take a bit of profit off the book." He says the general outlook in the market is more uncertain than bullish, pointing to the likely refinancing of large reported Chinese copper inventories in the coming months as a factor that could pressure the metal in the long-term. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to fall, tracking losses in most Asian markets amid concerns over the nuclear situation in Japan, traders say. They expect the main index to test the support level of 3720 after it ended up 0.1% at 3745.838 Monday. "A drop in crude oil prices could also inspire a selloff in most commodity-related stocks, but expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its key interest rate unchanged at 6.75% later in the session may trigger bargain-buying in financial-related stocks," says a trader with a foreign securities firm. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK), tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) may lead falls on profit-taking, but Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may gain on bargain-buying. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 04-11-11 2235ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110412e74c0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
