\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Shirakawa: G-7  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Helped Prevent Spillover Into Other Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 201 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2011\par 12:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Monday that the Group of Seven major nation's pledge of joint foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's surge in the days after a massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11 was key to limiting financial market damage.\par \par \uc2 "Coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 countries played a critical role in not only stabilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but also helping to prevent negative spillovers into other markets, for example Japan's stock market," Shirakawa said in a speech in Tokyo on Monday evening.\par \par \uc2 The yen has weakened since the G-7 pledged joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 18. The dollar traded around Y84.63 Monday evening in Tokyo, up sharply from the post World War II-low of Y76.25 hit on March 17.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yen helps Japan's key manufacturing sector by making exports less expensive overseas and boosting overseas revenue sent back to Japan.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com [ 04-11-11 0628ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110411e74b0001y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
