\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Yen Slide Could Prove Brief As Hedge Funds Exit Bearish Bets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 968 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2011\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors may be getting carried away with dumping the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened over 10% since the world's central banks acted to curb its sudden spike to a high of Y76.25 per dollar, yet some traders and hedge funds are warning that any deeper slide may prove short-lived, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the yen recovered, hitting Y84.50 per dollar from about Y85.50, after a new 7.1-magnitude quake rocked Japan, briefly sending investors out of risky bets like stocks and into assets perceived as safer, like the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro, which hit an 11-month high against the yen this week, lost 0.9%. Such moves suggest the yen's retreat may halt.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 "I don't think this move toward a weaker yen has legs," says George Papamarkakis of London-based hedge fund \uc2 North Asset Management LLP\uc2 , which manages roughly $200 million in assets. "There's no real underlying flow supporting the move and it's just become self-fulfilling."\par \par \uc2 In mid-March, when the yen soared in value to under Y80 per dollar amid expectations that Japanese investors and companies would repatriate money to cover costs arising from that month's 9.0-magnitude earthquake and tsunami, Mr. Papamarkakis used options contracts to bet against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and also sold the yen outright. Both wagers helped him hedge other positions in his portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Papamarkakis figured that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would eventually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail the yen's strength, which hurts Japanese companies by making their exports costlier abroad. That move came March 18, with the U.S. Federal Reserve and other central banks adding their weight to the first globally-coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2000. The yen slid.\par \par \uc2 But while the yen's slide to Y85 has surprised Mr. Papamarkakis--he was expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop around Y83 or Y84--he's confident it won't continue. Around 11 a.m. in London on Thursday, he exited his bearish trades, with the yen at around Y85.\par \par \uc2 For years, hedge-fund managers like Kyle Bass of Hayman Advisors LP in Dallas and \uc2 David Einhorn\uc2 's \uc2 Greenlight Capital Inc\uc2 . have bet against Japan, saying the country's moribund economy, shrinking population and massive national debt--the highest among industrialized nations as a proportion of gross domestic product--will eventually spark a financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Now, yen bears are saying that Japan's rebuilding costs could push the country's finances deeper into the red. That could raise the country's financing rates, compounding its debt problems. To help the economy recover, the Bank of Japan has also been printing money, a policy called "quantitative easing" that has been diluting the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 But betting against Japan has long been a losing proposition. Unlike the U.S., Japan is a creditor to other countries, with a large current account and trade surplus. Roughly 95% of its government debt is owned by Japanese investors, making the country less vulnerable to a Greece-style crisis.\par \par \uc2 In a sign that investors are less worried, prices of credit-default swaps that insure against a Japanese debt default have fallen roughly 25% in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 As a result, it's not clear that long-time Japan bears are substantially increasing their negative bets, observers say.\par \par \uc2 "It's too early to make that call," says Robert Bogucki, managing director at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, macroeconomics hedge funds like \uc2 North Asset Management\uc2  and computer-driven "trend-following" quantitative funds partly responsible for the yen's slide may be moving to take profits.\par \par \uc2 There are several other reasons to be skeptical about further yen weakness, observers say.\par \par \uc2 First, central banks' recent moves don't support a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some traders were expecting the Bank of Japan to expand its quantitative easing efforts to help the Japanese economy on Thursday, but that didn't happen, giving the yen a lift.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet did not clearly signal that the bank's much-anticipated hike in interest rates--the first among major economies since 2008--will be followed by another in May. Brazil, for its part, announced more measures to curb inflows of capital, partly because of carry trades involving selling the yen and buying the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These moves boosted the Japanese yen at the expense of the dollar, euro and Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 Some investors have been emboldened by recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fiscal year to take options bets against the yen and reinstate the so-called carry trade, which involves borrowing and selling the yen and using the proceeds to buy higher-yielding assets like the Australian dollar and Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 But recent data suggest Japanese firms may finally be starting to bring cash home from abroad to help pay for the disaster--a phenomenon missing from the last few weeks. That source of yen-buying could support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "One slightly worrying sign for Japanese yen bears is that Japanese investors sold JPY 1.1 trillion of foreign debt in the latest reporting week, hinting at a large repatriation," notes Chris Turner, a London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Dutch bank ING.\par \par \uc2 That suggests that major weakness in the yen could actually be a ways off. "There's no doubt that Japan's external position is weakening," says \uc2 North Asset Management\uc2 's Mr. Papamarkakis. "But it's going to take a very long time before that plays out." [ 04-07-11 1708ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110407e747000w7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
